169
00:08:23,290 --> 00:08:26,230
In this lecture, I will talk
about inference time techniques

170
00:08:26,230 --> 00:08:27,380
for LLM reasoning.

171
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:29,470
So in the intro
of the course, we

172
00:08:29,470 --> 00:08:31,030
will briefly talk
about [? things ?]

173
00:08:31,030 --> 00:08:33,549
like advancement of
reasoning models, which

174
00:08:33,549 --> 00:08:36,070
is kind of one of the highlights
of large language model

175
00:08:36,070 --> 00:08:38,049
development in last year.

176
00:08:38,049 --> 00:08:40,840
And so here I will
go more into details

177
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:43,960
about what we can see from
these reasoning models.

178
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:46,560
So on the slides
I'm showing this,

179
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:50,470
like a result, which was
quoted from the o1 blog.

180
00:08:50,470 --> 00:08:54,450
So from here, we can see that
actually OpenAI o1 model started

181
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:58,290
to achieve pretty impressive
performance across various very

182
00:08:58,290 --> 00:09:00,960
challenging tasks where
previous models are really

183
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:02,050
struggling with.

184
00:09:02,050 --> 00:09:05,170
So this includes the
competition level math problems,

185
00:09:05,170 --> 00:09:07,660
like in the AIME
contest last year,

186
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:10,596
and also competitive programming
contest on Codeforces.

187
00:09:13,470 --> 00:09:17,280
And in the recent o3 results,
they further showed this.

188
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:20,430
The performance can further
improve with more inference time

189
00:09:20,430 --> 00:09:21,450
compute.

190
00:09:21,450 --> 00:09:24,280
For example, here I'm
showing these curves.

191
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:28,090
They demonstrated on a
benchmark called ARC-AGI.

192
00:09:28,090 --> 00:09:31,110
So this is a benchmark that
includes a bunch of puzzles

193
00:09:31,110 --> 00:09:36,450
to test how well humans or AI
models can learn some reasoning

194
00:09:36,450 --> 00:09:38,920
patterns, given the
demonstration examples.

195
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:41,170
So this was a very
challenging task.

196
00:09:41,170 --> 00:09:44,330
So for those existing
models before o1,

197
00:09:44,330 --> 00:09:47,680
if we do not apply any special
inference type techniques

198
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:50,600
and just do the very
regular inference,

199
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:54,580
then all existing models
can achieve a less than 25%

200
00:09:54,580 --> 00:09:55,510
accuracy.

201
00:09:55,510 --> 00:10:00,080
Even with o1 model, it can only
achieve around 30% accuracy.

202
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:04,360
But with the recent o3 model,
if we set the inference time,

203
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:08,210
like a budget to
around $20 per task,

204
00:10:08,210 --> 00:10:10,540
it can achieve a
performance that kind

205
00:10:10,540 --> 00:10:13,210
of matching the average
human annotators

206
00:10:13,210 --> 00:10:14,600
or mechanical turkers.

207
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:18,190
And if we further scale out this
inference time cost to a crazy

208
00:10:18,190 --> 00:10:21,350
number like $1,000
to solve one problem,

209
00:10:21,350 --> 00:10:24,890
then actually the model
can achieve 87.5% accuracy.

210
00:10:24,890 --> 00:10:27,820
So definitely this is
a very expensive way

211
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:30,700
to solve this
particular task, but it

212
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:34,400
shows how this model can
perform in the ideal case

213
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:37,550
when we do not care so much
about inference time compute.

214
00:10:37,550 --> 00:10:41,550
Then here on the slides, I'm
showing a very simple OpenAI o1

215
00:10:41,550 --> 00:10:43,920
demo will basically
cause the model

216
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:46,140
to solve a planning problem.

217
00:10:46,140 --> 00:10:49,440
So what is different
for this o1 model?

218
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:52,560
So the main difference
is that, for the existing

219
00:10:52,560 --> 00:10:57,360
LLM before the o1 model, when
we enter a user query then

220
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:59,800
the model will directly
generate this response.

221
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:03,700
But in the o1 interface, they
include this thought process.

222
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:07,530
So basically this process can
take different amount of time,

223
00:11:07,530 --> 00:11:09,960
depending on the
difficulty of the task.

224
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:12,760
For example, for the specific
question I asked the model,

225
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:16,170
it takes a slightly
more than one minute

226
00:11:16,170 --> 00:11:19,080
to generate the final solution.

227
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:23,670
And in the o1 interface, they
didn't show the raw thought

228
00:11:23,670 --> 00:11:24,910
process.

229
00:11:24,910 --> 00:11:28,530
Instead, they show a summarized
version of the hidden thought.

230
00:11:28,530 --> 00:11:31,960
So from this hidden thought for
this specific planning problem,

231
00:11:31,960 --> 00:11:35,460
we can see that it summarizes
some key stages in the reasoning

232
00:11:35,460 --> 00:11:38,710
process, including laying
out an initial plan,

233
00:11:38,710 --> 00:11:42,650
evaluating it based on the
model's internal belief,

234
00:11:42,650 --> 00:11:47,650
and then revise the plan so that
it can satisfy the constraints

235
00:11:47,650 --> 00:11:51,640
and improve the optimality.

236
00:11:51,640 --> 00:11:55,670
Then, with the Gemini
2.0 thinking model,

237
00:11:55,670 --> 00:12:00,310
we also make this sort
tokens visible to users.

238
00:12:00,310 --> 00:12:04,840
So on slides, I'm showing a demo
where actually this question

239
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:06,800
includes an image input.

240
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:08,800
So this question is--

241
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:11,030
the math part of this
question is pretty simple.

242
00:12:11,030 --> 00:12:14,150
So basically, there are
four balls in the image,

243
00:12:14,150 --> 00:12:17,080
and the question is asking
the model to take three out

244
00:12:17,080 --> 00:12:21,550
of [? list ?] [? of ?]
numbers so that the sum is 30.

245
00:12:21,550 --> 00:12:24,620
And so the thought here, as
you can see, is a pretty long.

246
00:12:24,620 --> 00:12:27,220
So after we enter the
query, the model immediately

247
00:12:27,220 --> 00:12:28,640
generates these thoughts.

248
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:30,880
And we don't need to read
the reasoning process

249
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:31,730
token by token.

250
00:12:31,730 --> 00:12:35,530
I know it's pretty hard to read,
but the most interesting step we

251
00:12:35,530 --> 00:12:38,730
can see from this multi-modal
query is queries that there is

252
00:12:38,730 --> 00:12:39,480
a--

253
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:42,720
so at the beginning
of this image,

254
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:46,300
there is a specific
number, which is 9.

255
00:12:46,300 --> 00:12:48,600
So at some point,
the model figures out

256
00:12:48,600 --> 00:12:50,950
a very important step
for this question.

257
00:12:50,950 --> 00:12:54,240
So because if you want
to get the sum of 30,

258
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:57,640
actually, we need to turn this
9 into 6 by rolling this ball.

259
00:12:57,640 --> 00:13:00,330
So this is a very
interesting feature

260
00:13:00,330 --> 00:13:05,010
of this query when we put
these numbers on some balls.

261
00:13:05,010 --> 00:13:10,470
And also, this
step itself is not

262
00:13:10,470 --> 00:13:14,580
that kind of difficult
mathematically, but actually

263
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:15,970
it is an interesting trick.

264
00:13:15,970 --> 00:13:18,750
And if we-- earlier,
if we do not

265
00:13:18,750 --> 00:13:20,830
allow the model to
generate this long thought,

266
00:13:20,830 --> 00:13:23,650
this specific step will be
very hard to figure out.

267
00:13:23,650 --> 00:13:29,760
And also like-- besides these
models from OpenAI and Google

268
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:32,040
DeepMind, there are
other reasoning models

269
00:13:32,040 --> 00:13:33,475
out there recently.

270
00:13:33,475 --> 00:13:35,350
At the beginning of the
lecture, I mentioned,

271
00:13:35,350 --> 00:13:38,320
there are also models from
the DeepSeek team and also

272
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:39,550
the Kimi team.

273
00:13:39,550 --> 00:13:41,890
And out of all
these breakthrough

274
00:13:41,890 --> 00:13:44,830
in reasoning models,
one core shared idea

275
00:13:44,830 --> 00:13:47,590
is that we need some way to
trigger the large language

276
00:13:47,590 --> 00:13:49,840
model to generate
long chain of thought

277
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:53,650
before it actually concludes
with the final solution.

278
00:13:53,650 --> 00:13:55,840
In the literature, there
are different approaches

279
00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:58,720
to trigger this
train-of-thought generation.

280
00:13:58,720 --> 00:14:00,980
Like in the original
chain of thought paper,

281
00:14:00,980 --> 00:14:03,340
they designed a
few-shot prompting

282
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:06,160
scheme, which basically
demonstrates this thought

283
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:08,860
process in exemplars.

284
00:14:08,860 --> 00:14:10,390
And later on, there
are some works

285
00:14:10,390 --> 00:14:13,030
showing that we can apply
an instruction prompting

286
00:14:13,030 --> 00:14:14,060
to achieve this.

287
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:15,550
Like, we can use
the instruction,

288
00:14:15,550 --> 00:14:19,060
let's think step by step.

289
00:14:19,060 --> 00:14:21,380
And for the reason
that chat models,

290
00:14:21,380 --> 00:14:23,290
we can see that
even if we do not

291
00:14:23,290 --> 00:14:25,940
give any special
instructions to the model,

292
00:14:25,940 --> 00:14:28,990
the model also tends to generate
some thoughts before coming up

293
00:14:28,990 --> 00:14:32,020
with the final answer, no
matter whether the thought is

294
00:14:32,020 --> 00:14:33,140
short or long.

295
00:14:33,140 --> 00:14:36,840
So there are different ways to
train the model to achieve this.

296
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:39,020
Like in for instruction
tuning, where

297
00:14:39,020 --> 00:14:41,420
we actually put this
train-of-thought data

298
00:14:41,420 --> 00:14:44,120
into our supervised
fine tuning mixture.

299
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:47,580
And also what we can see
from recent reasoning models,

300
00:14:47,580 --> 00:14:50,125
we can apply reinforcement
learning to achieve this goal.

301
00:14:52,850 --> 00:14:55,190
In this lecture,
I will focus more

302
00:14:55,190 --> 00:14:58,530
on inference time techniques
for scaling token budget.

303
00:14:58,530 --> 00:15:02,510
And in the future lectures,
we will also dive deeper

304
00:15:02,510 --> 00:15:03,900
into reasoning techniques.

305
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:05,870
And there will be
some other guest

306
00:15:05,870 --> 00:15:08,140
speakers who will come here
to share their thoughts.

307
00:15:11,330 --> 00:15:14,000
So for this lecture,
I will discuss

308
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,420
three parts about reasoning
techniques at inference time.

309
00:15:17,420 --> 00:15:20,780
I will first discuss the
basic prompting techniques.

310
00:15:20,780 --> 00:15:23,270
And the common idea among
these prompting techniques

311
00:15:23,270 --> 00:15:25,280
is that we want
to allow the model

312
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:30,440
to use more token budget to
generate a single solution.

313
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:32,890
Then the second part, I
will talk about techniques

314
00:15:32,890 --> 00:15:36,820
to perform search and selection
from multiple candidates.

315
00:15:36,820 --> 00:15:38,710
And the common idea
here is that if you

316
00:15:38,710 --> 00:15:43,870
want to increase the width to
explore the solution space.

317
00:15:43,870 --> 00:15:47,350
In the last part, I will talk
about iterative self-improvement

318
00:15:47,350 --> 00:15:49,690
will allow the model
to increase its depth

319
00:15:49,690 --> 00:15:52,780
to reach the final solution.

320
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:55,250
Then, after all these
discussions of different parts,

321
00:15:55,250 --> 00:15:59,740
we will also see how we can best
utilize different combinations

322
00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:02,920
of these techniques
to seek the best

323
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:06,310
way to utilize the inference
time compute to improve the LLM

324
00:16:06,310 --> 00:16:09,370
reasoning performance.

325
00:16:09,370 --> 00:16:11,650
So let's get started
on the first part

326
00:16:11,650 --> 00:16:15,700
about basic
prompting techniques.

327
00:16:15,700 --> 00:16:18,500
Before we talk about more
advanced prompting techniques,

328
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:20,200
let's first review
the background

329
00:16:20,200 --> 00:16:22,270
about standard prompting.

330
00:16:22,270 --> 00:16:24,375
So basically in
standard prompting,

331
00:16:24,375 --> 00:16:25,750
let's say we want
to solve a math

332
00:16:25,750 --> 00:16:29,110
problem as shown on the slides,
before the line of works

333
00:16:29,110 --> 00:16:32,120
on chain-of-thought prompting,
the common practice is

334
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:35,000
that we just put some
question-answer pairs

335
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:36,050
into the prompt.

336
00:16:36,050 --> 00:16:36,960
Like here.

337
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:38,460
The question is
the math question,

338
00:16:38,460 --> 00:16:40,880
and the answer is
the final answer.

339
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:44,490
So before the advancement
in post-training techniques,

340
00:16:44,490 --> 00:16:46,950
if we directly apply
the standard prompting,

341
00:16:46,950 --> 00:16:51,320
then the performance is pretty
poor on reasoning benchmarks.

342
00:16:51,320 --> 00:16:53,270
The fundamental
issue is that if we

343
00:16:53,270 --> 00:16:55,410
look at this standard
future exemplars,

344
00:16:55,410 --> 00:16:58,860
they only provide information
on the final solution format,

345
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:01,340
but it doesn't tell the
model about the rationale

346
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:02,940
to derive the solution.

347
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:06,470
Now we can imagine that
if we are taking a class

348
00:17:06,470 --> 00:17:09,319
and the teachers are
basically just teaching you

349
00:17:09,319 --> 00:17:12,619
with the final, like official
solution without any more

350
00:17:12,619 --> 00:17:16,550
context, then if you do not have
a relative background knowledge

351
00:17:16,550 --> 00:17:22,490
yourself, then it will make your
teaching a lot more difficult.

352
00:17:22,490 --> 00:17:24,750
So for the
chain-of-thought prompting,

353
00:17:24,750 --> 00:17:28,840
the idea is that we will include
these few short exemplars

354
00:17:28,840 --> 00:17:30,430
with sorts.

355
00:17:30,430 --> 00:17:32,830
Again, let's take a
look at this example

356
00:17:32,830 --> 00:17:34,490
of solving math problems.

357
00:17:34,490 --> 00:17:36,940
So here, instead
in exemplars, we

358
00:17:36,940 --> 00:17:40,150
will provide this calculation
steps to basically demonstrate

359
00:17:40,150 --> 00:17:46,600
the model how we should derive
this solution step by step

360
00:17:46,600 --> 00:17:48,920
to achieve the final answer.

361
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:52,810
Then after giving this
exemplars with chain-of-thought

362
00:17:52,810 --> 00:17:56,020
rationales, then the model
is able to solve this problem

363
00:17:56,020 --> 00:17:56,840
correctly.

364
00:17:59,770 --> 00:18:04,210
In practice, how these
models can benefit more

365
00:18:04,210 --> 00:18:07,420
from chain-of-thought prompting
compared to standard prompting

366
00:18:07,420 --> 00:18:11,470
is also dependent on the model's
fundamental capabilities.

367
00:18:11,470 --> 00:18:13,390
So here on the
slides, I'm showing

368
00:18:13,390 --> 00:18:16,510
the scaling curves presented by
the original chain-of-thought

369
00:18:16,510 --> 00:18:19,210
papers and also some
other related papers

370
00:18:19,210 --> 00:18:23,050
about this chain-of-thought
properties.

371
00:18:23,050 --> 00:18:26,900
So we can see that among the
different model families,

372
00:18:26,900 --> 00:18:30,230
one common trend is that
this CoT performance improves

373
00:18:30,230 --> 00:18:35,210
more significantly with the
increase of the model size.

374
00:18:35,210 --> 00:18:37,610
In particular, if
we look at this gap

375
00:18:37,610 --> 00:18:40,410
of chain-of-thought prompting
versus standard prompting,

376
00:18:40,410 --> 00:18:43,740
then better models benefit
more with CoT generation,

377
00:18:43,740 --> 00:18:45,980
and there, we
suggest improvement

378
00:18:45,980 --> 00:18:48,290
on reasoning performance
once the model

379
00:18:48,290 --> 00:18:51,105
reaches a certain scale.

380
00:18:51,105 --> 00:18:54,690
And I also want to note
that here for these curves,

381
00:18:54,690 --> 00:18:57,390
they are from papers in 2022.

382
00:18:57,390 --> 00:19:01,590
So these experiments are done
using pre-trained only LLMs.

383
00:19:01,590 --> 00:19:03,950
So this is why it is
important to apply

384
00:19:03,950 --> 00:19:06,410
some prompting so that
the model can generate

385
00:19:06,410 --> 00:19:08,930
the results immediately.

386
00:19:08,930 --> 00:19:11,510
And also with this recent
post-training techniques,

387
00:19:11,510 --> 00:19:13,790
they might show some
different scaling curves

388
00:19:13,790 --> 00:19:17,330
like we have seen some
strong but very lightweight

389
00:19:17,330 --> 00:19:18,660
LLMs recently.

390
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:21,990
But the main conclusions
will still hold.

391
00:19:21,990 --> 00:19:23,880
We will still see
this overall trend.

392
00:19:23,880 --> 00:19:26,750
Basically, once the
model becomes better,

393
00:19:26,750 --> 00:19:30,010
it will benefit more
from this allowance

394
00:19:30,010 --> 00:19:31,670
of the train-of-thought
generation.

395
00:19:31,670 --> 00:19:35,050
Then in the follow up work
called large language models

396
00:19:35,050 --> 00:19:36,620
are zero-shot reasoners.

397
00:19:36,620 --> 00:19:39,760
They further find that even with
pre-trained only large language

398
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:43,600
models, we do not have to
provide a model with exemplars

399
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:46,240
to elicit these CoT generation.

400
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:48,970
Instead, we can achieve
this with an instruction.

401
00:19:48,970 --> 00:19:51,530
Like in the paper, they
demonstrate this instruction.

402
00:19:51,530 --> 00:19:52,910
Let's think step by step.

403
00:19:52,910 --> 00:19:55,160
Then, after we apply
this instruction,

404
00:19:55,160 --> 00:19:57,830
before we ask the model
to generate the answer,

405
00:19:57,830 --> 00:20:00,100
the model can also
generate some thoughts

406
00:20:00,100 --> 00:20:01,695
before generating
the final solution.

407
00:20:04,570 --> 00:20:06,370
And they empirically
they demonstrate

408
00:20:06,370 --> 00:20:08,740
that this zero-shot
CoT significantly

409
00:20:08,740 --> 00:20:11,470
outperforms zero-shot
performance, especially

410
00:20:11,470 --> 00:20:15,340
on harder, like, mass and
symbolic reasoning tasks.

411
00:20:15,340 --> 00:20:19,700
So zero-shot CoT is more
convenient than few-shot CoT

412
00:20:19,700 --> 00:20:22,990
in the sense that we no longer
need to manually annotate

413
00:20:22,990 --> 00:20:25,740
source ourselves as exemplars.

414
00:20:25,740 --> 00:20:28,260
But if we compare
their performance,

415
00:20:28,260 --> 00:20:30,120
zero-shot CoT
performance is still

416
00:20:30,120 --> 00:20:32,820
much worse than few-shot CoT.

417
00:20:32,820 --> 00:20:35,340
Then, a very natural
question I want to ask

418
00:20:35,340 --> 00:20:38,650
is, how can we get the
best of both worlds?

419
00:20:38,650 --> 00:20:44,580
How to CoT performance without
manually labeling samplers?

420
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:46,200
So this is what
we want to achieve

421
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:50,170
with our paper, large language
models, as analogical reasoners.

422
00:20:50,170 --> 00:20:53,170
And this paper was
published at ICLR last year.

423
00:20:53,170 --> 00:20:56,310
The idea is that, instead of
directly providing the model

424
00:20:56,310 --> 00:20:58,950
with exemplars, we
instruct the model

425
00:20:58,950 --> 00:21:01,410
to first record
relevant exemplars

426
00:21:01,410 --> 00:21:03,340
to solve the test problem.

427
00:21:03,340 --> 00:21:06,460
Then, based on this model
generated examplars,

428
00:21:06,460 --> 00:21:09,750
then the model will try to
solve the test problems.

429
00:21:09,750 --> 00:21:13,530
Just imagine that these are
self-generated exemplars

430
00:21:13,530 --> 00:21:17,735
are just some CoT examples
provided by external sources.

431
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:24,150
The benefit of this
design lies in two folds.

432
00:21:24,150 --> 00:21:26,670
First, these exemplars
are self-generated

433
00:21:26,670 --> 00:21:31,300
by the large language models,
so we fulfill our original goal.

434
00:21:31,300 --> 00:21:34,890
We don't need to do any
manual labeling here.

435
00:21:34,890 --> 00:21:37,110
On the other hand,
because this instruction

436
00:21:37,110 --> 00:21:39,430
is applied to each
test question,

437
00:21:39,430 --> 00:21:42,010
so basically for
different test question,

438
00:21:42,010 --> 00:21:44,850
the model is able to generate
different exemplars which

439
00:21:44,850 --> 00:21:47,280
are more relevant to the
concrete topics covered

440
00:21:47,280 --> 00:21:48,400
in the test question.

441
00:21:48,400 --> 00:21:52,650
So these exemplars are more
tailored to individual problems.

442
00:21:52,650 --> 00:21:55,210
We call our approach
analogical prompting,

443
00:21:55,210 --> 00:21:57,810
because this
methodology is actually

444
00:21:57,810 --> 00:22:01,740
can be motivated by human
analogical reasoning.

445
00:22:01,740 --> 00:22:03,940
If we think about
few-shot prompting,

446
00:22:03,940 --> 00:22:06,870
it is not supernatural
if we think about it

447
00:22:06,870 --> 00:22:09,630
in the human reasoning
way, because humans are not

448
00:22:09,630 --> 00:22:12,270
explicitly given
demonstrations every time

449
00:22:12,270 --> 00:22:15,600
for each single new problem.

450
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:18,660
And instead, humans also
intrinsically recall

451
00:22:18,660 --> 00:22:21,260
from past relevant experience.

452
00:22:21,260 --> 00:22:23,840
So in a classic reasoning
methodology book

453
00:22:23,840 --> 00:22:26,130
called How to Solve
It from Polya,

454
00:22:26,130 --> 00:22:29,090
they have a very nice
description about this reasoning

455
00:22:29,090 --> 00:22:32,100
method named, do
a related problem?

456
00:22:32,100 --> 00:22:35,150
So the idea is that
when solving a problem,

457
00:22:35,150 --> 00:22:38,150
we look for a pre-formally
solved problem, which

458
00:22:38,150 --> 00:22:42,570
is linked to our present one by
generalization, specialization,

459
00:22:42,570 --> 00:22:43,620
or analogy.

460
00:22:43,620 --> 00:22:48,330
And we want to learn from these
past results or their methods,

461
00:22:48,330 --> 00:22:51,835
so that it can help us solve
new task problems better.

462
00:22:55,160 --> 00:22:58,470
If we think about it from the
human reasoning perspective,

463
00:22:58,470 --> 00:23:01,010
then besides
exemplars, we can also

464
00:23:01,010 --> 00:23:03,830
instruct a model to
generate some higher level

465
00:23:03,830 --> 00:23:08,270
knowledge, which can be a
more better summary of what we

466
00:23:08,270 --> 00:23:10,170
need to solve the new problems.

467
00:23:10,170 --> 00:23:13,070
And this generated knowledge
can complement the problems

468
00:23:13,070 --> 00:23:15,080
with broader insights.

469
00:23:15,080 --> 00:23:20,250
So here, let's take solving
coding problems as an example.

470
00:23:20,250 --> 00:23:22,500
So given a new
coding problem, we

471
00:23:22,500 --> 00:23:25,170
can first instruct the
model to self generate

472
00:23:25,170 --> 00:23:27,870
some knowledge, which
is more of higher level

473
00:23:27,870 --> 00:23:31,240
tutorial about the algorithms
we need to solve the problem.

474
00:23:31,240 --> 00:23:33,030
And also, the model
needs to identify

475
00:23:33,030 --> 00:23:35,880
what are the main algorithms
we need so that it can come up

476
00:23:35,880 --> 00:23:38,100
with related
examples of problems

477
00:23:38,100 --> 00:23:40,620
with their corresponding code.

478
00:23:40,620 --> 00:23:44,910
Then after this instruction, and
also once the model generated

479
00:23:44,910 --> 00:23:48,150
has related a background
knowledge including higher level

480
00:23:48,150 --> 00:23:50,760
tutorial and exemplars,
then the model

481
00:23:50,760 --> 00:23:54,180
goes back to solve the
initial test problem.

482
00:23:54,180 --> 00:23:56,700
So in the results, we show
that basically analogical

483
00:23:56,700 --> 00:23:59,760
prompting not only
outperforms zero-shot CoT,

484
00:23:59,760 --> 00:24:03,130
but it actually outperforms
manual few-shot CoT as well.

485
00:24:03,130 --> 00:24:07,080
And we show this performance
improvement on a math problem

486
00:24:07,080 --> 00:24:09,240
solving, code
generation, and also

487
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:11,970
like BIG-Bench reasoning tasks.

488
00:24:11,970 --> 00:24:14,740
If you look at these
results at the first time,

489
00:24:14,740 --> 00:24:17,940
you might be a bit surprised
because knowledge here,

490
00:24:17,940 --> 00:24:20,030
all these examples of
problems or knowledge,

491
00:24:20,030 --> 00:24:21,600
are generated by the model.

492
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:25,400
So intuitively it might
contain more errors

493
00:24:25,400 --> 00:24:28,170
than human annotated exemplars.

494
00:24:28,170 --> 00:24:30,750
Empirically, we indeed
observe this trend.

495
00:24:30,750 --> 00:24:33,830
We see that, for example, for
low stack exemplars generated

496
00:24:33,830 --> 00:24:38,310
by the model for math problems,
around 70% of them are accurate,

497
00:24:38,310 --> 00:24:39,780
and also they are relevant.

498
00:24:39,780 --> 00:24:42,950
But the rest can
have some issues,

499
00:24:42,950 --> 00:24:45,860
they are completely irrelevant,
or there are some actually wrong

500
00:24:45,860 --> 00:24:47,360
calculation steps.

501
00:24:47,360 --> 00:24:50,760
But even so, there are some
noises in the demonstrations.

502
00:24:50,760 --> 00:24:52,780
The model still
benefits a lot from it.

503
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:00,560
So given this like a finding,
you might imagine that this

504
00:25:00,560 --> 00:25:02,960
means at least the model
should be able to generate some

505
00:25:02,960 --> 00:25:05,810
reasonable exemplars, or
otherwise it cannot learn from

506
00:25:05,810 --> 00:25:06,830
it at all.

507
00:25:06,830 --> 00:25:09,290
So based on this
intuition, we also

508
00:25:09,290 --> 00:25:12,950
conduct a pretty
rough scaling study

509
00:25:12,950 --> 00:25:15,240
for this analogical prompting.

510
00:25:15,240 --> 00:25:19,590
So here we are comparing
models among different--

511
00:25:19,590 --> 00:25:22,090
among the GPT text
model families.

512
00:25:22,090 --> 00:25:25,210
I know these models are all
shut down and deprecated now,

513
00:25:25,210 --> 00:25:28,380
but this trend will still hold.

514
00:25:28,380 --> 00:25:31,470
So basically, we can see
that weaker large language

515
00:25:31,470 --> 00:25:34,720
models benefit less from
analogical prompting.

516
00:25:34,720 --> 00:25:36,840
Although if we compare
the performance

517
00:25:36,840 --> 00:25:41,070
to zero-shot
train-of-thought, it is on par

518
00:25:41,070 --> 00:25:45,090
or still outperforms
their performance.

519
00:25:45,090 --> 00:25:47,490
And with stronger
language models,

520
00:25:47,490 --> 00:25:50,910
starting from text adventure
002 in the GPT family,

521
00:25:50,910 --> 00:25:53,580
analogical prompting starts
to outperform chain-of-thought

522
00:25:53,580 --> 00:25:56,580
prompting with manually
designed exemplars.

523
00:25:56,580 --> 00:25:59,580
And notice that actually,
this approach can even

524
00:25:59,580 --> 00:26:01,660
outperform retrieved exemplars.

525
00:26:01,660 --> 00:26:03,780
So for retrieval
method, what we did

526
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:06,040
is that basically for
each test problem,

527
00:26:06,040 --> 00:26:07,890
we implement a
retrieval approach

528
00:26:07,890 --> 00:26:11,430
so that it will find those
most relevant problems

529
00:26:11,430 --> 00:26:16,280
in the least like GSM8K
mass corpus training data.

530
00:26:16,280 --> 00:26:20,180
So one explanation here
is that this generated

531
00:26:20,180 --> 00:26:22,850
train-of-thought by the
model can be more tailored

532
00:26:22,850 --> 00:26:25,740
to the underlying
reasoning style of these,

533
00:26:25,740 --> 00:26:29,510
like pre-trained or instruction
tuned large language models.

534
00:26:29,510 --> 00:26:33,660
So this finding actually is also
in line with the recent trend,

535
00:26:33,660 --> 00:26:37,310
when we are trying to develop
the reasoning models instead

536
00:26:37,310 --> 00:26:41,220
of putting human
written thoughts,

537
00:26:41,220 --> 00:26:42,620
we can have some
training methods

538
00:26:42,620 --> 00:26:47,420
so that the model is able to
discover the best like reasoning

539
00:26:47,420 --> 00:26:50,150
strategies by itself.

540
00:26:50,150 --> 00:26:52,280
Now let's go back
to the instruction.

541
00:26:52,280 --> 00:26:54,110
Let's think step by step.

542
00:26:54,110 --> 00:26:57,030
So why do we have to
use this sentence?

543
00:26:57,030 --> 00:26:59,720
Is there something
special about it?

544
00:26:59,720 --> 00:27:02,310
So in the original
zero-shot CoT paper,

545
00:27:02,310 --> 00:27:03,890
they conducted
this ablation study

546
00:27:03,890 --> 00:27:06,230
of different variants
of these instructions

547
00:27:06,230 --> 00:27:08,220
to trigger the
train-of-thought generation.

548
00:27:08,220 --> 00:27:10,620
So some of them do
not make much sense.

549
00:27:10,620 --> 00:27:15,280
So it is, not surprising that
they do not perform well.

550
00:27:15,280 --> 00:27:17,640
But if we look at those
different instructions

551
00:27:17,640 --> 00:27:19,930
in the top category,
instructive,

552
00:27:19,930 --> 00:27:22,660
If we mask out this
accuracy numbers,

553
00:27:22,660 --> 00:27:26,430
it might be not that intuitive
know which instruction

554
00:27:26,430 --> 00:27:28,120
should be performed better.

555
00:27:28,120 --> 00:27:31,890
And it can be kind of
not very good to see

556
00:27:31,890 --> 00:27:34,840
that even if you just
change a couple of words,

557
00:27:34,840 --> 00:27:37,590
then the accuracy number can
change very dramatically.

558
00:27:37,590 --> 00:27:40,710
So this shows that current
large models are pretty

559
00:27:40,710 --> 00:27:43,380
sensitive to prompt design.

560
00:27:43,380 --> 00:27:46,240
And because-- and
also in many cases,

561
00:27:46,240 --> 00:27:48,480
there is no clear
principle about how

562
00:27:48,480 --> 00:27:50,020
to write optimal performance.

563
00:27:50,020 --> 00:27:52,170
And this is why
nowadays there are still

564
00:27:52,170 --> 00:27:54,990
many people who are
writing a lot of guidelines

565
00:27:54,990 --> 00:27:58,020
about how to have the
best practice to do

566
00:27:58,020 --> 00:28:00,420
prompt engineering.

567
00:28:00,420 --> 00:28:04,080
So given this observation,
a very natural question

568
00:28:04,080 --> 00:28:07,620
I want to ask next is, how we
can reduce this manual work

569
00:28:07,620 --> 00:28:08,660
for writing prompts?

570
00:28:12,280 --> 00:28:14,950
So one idea is that
since our large models

571
00:28:14,950 --> 00:28:18,970
can solve many challenging
reasoning problems

572
00:28:18,970 --> 00:28:20,720
and also answering
other queries,

573
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:23,200
so why don't we
also use it to help

574
00:28:23,200 --> 00:28:26,450
us do the prompt engineering
or prompt design in general?

575
00:28:26,450 --> 00:28:31,630
So in ICLR 2023 there is a paper
called Large Language Models are

576
00:28:31,630 --> 00:28:33,680
Human Level Prompt Engineers.

577
00:28:33,680 --> 00:28:35,470
So the high level
idea is basically

578
00:28:35,470 --> 00:28:39,310
to apply a large model
to propose prompts,

579
00:28:39,310 --> 00:28:41,410
so that we hope
this model generated

580
00:28:41,410 --> 00:28:44,710
prompts can also perform even
better than human written

581
00:28:44,710 --> 00:28:45,850
prompts.

582
00:28:45,850 --> 00:28:49,370
So here I'm showing LLM complete
diagram of their method.

583
00:28:49,370 --> 00:28:51,710
So there are two
important stages.

584
00:28:51,710 --> 00:28:54,190
The first is this the
proposal generation

585
00:28:54,190 --> 00:28:55,960
where we can leverage
this large language

586
00:28:55,960 --> 00:28:59,680
model to generate initial
instructions, given our task

587
00:28:59,680 --> 00:29:01,960
description.

588
00:29:01,960 --> 00:29:05,890
Then once the model generate
different proposed prompts,

589
00:29:05,890 --> 00:29:07,620
then we'll score
each instruction

590
00:29:07,620 --> 00:29:09,330
based on the
prediction correctness

591
00:29:09,330 --> 00:29:10,960
on a small set of problems.

592
00:29:10,960 --> 00:29:12,690
So here, basically
we will hold out

593
00:29:12,690 --> 00:29:15,810
a small validation
set, which can tell us

594
00:29:15,810 --> 00:29:18,240
the quality of each prompt.

595
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:21,690
So here the setup
might be more similar

596
00:29:21,690 --> 00:29:23,430
to the traditional
machine learning task

597
00:29:23,430 --> 00:29:26,920
where we have this separation
of training and test.

598
00:29:26,920 --> 00:29:32,370
But actually we do not need to
include so many examples here

599
00:29:32,370 --> 00:29:34,000
for this validation purpose.

600
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,780
Like in most cases, it
is sufficient to include

601
00:29:36,780 --> 00:29:41,910
tens of examples or just
around 100 problems.

602
00:29:41,910 --> 00:29:44,770
Then in our work published
at ICLR last year,

603
00:29:44,770 --> 00:29:48,750
we go further where we try to
use this [? language ?] model,

604
00:29:48,750 --> 00:29:52,260
not only just to propose
some instructions

605
00:29:52,260 --> 00:29:54,730
and do some kind of a
search and mutation,

606
00:29:54,730 --> 00:29:59,100
we also want to apply it as an
optimizer to iteratively improve

607
00:29:59,100 --> 00:30:00,780
the prompt.

608
00:30:00,780 --> 00:30:04,050
So the idea of our
approach is that we

609
00:30:04,050 --> 00:30:07,240
want to instruct the LLM to
leverage the past or partition

610
00:30:07,240 --> 00:30:10,700
trajectory represented as
sorted solution score pairs,

611
00:30:10,700 --> 00:30:13,480
so that it can learn
to iteratively generate

612
00:30:13,480 --> 00:30:16,990
better prompts with more steps.

613
00:30:16,990 --> 00:30:19,990
So in this work, we frame it
as a more general optimization

614
00:30:19,990 --> 00:30:22,010
framework in the
natural language space.

615
00:30:22,010 --> 00:30:25,520
But in this specific use
case of prompt optimization,

616
00:30:25,520 --> 00:30:27,130
basically this
means that we will

617
00:30:27,130 --> 00:30:31,270
have two large language models
as optimizers and evaluators.

618
00:30:31,270 --> 00:30:34,520
So basically this optimizer
is the language model,

619
00:30:34,520 --> 00:30:36,490
which is responsible
for proposing

620
00:30:36,490 --> 00:30:41,410
a new instruction, given old
ones and also the task samplers.

621
00:30:41,410 --> 00:30:43,340
And the evaluator
is the scholar,

622
00:30:43,340 --> 00:30:46,720
which will evaluate the accuracy
or performance of a given

623
00:30:46,720 --> 00:30:47,630
instruction.

624
00:30:50,740 --> 00:30:53,860
Now let's take a look at how
we can design a meta-prompt

625
00:30:53,860 --> 00:30:55,455
to achieve this goal.

626
00:30:55,455 --> 00:30:56,830
So on the slides,
I'm showing you

627
00:30:56,830 --> 00:30:59,050
an example,
meta-prompt for GSM8K

628
00:30:59,050 --> 00:31:01,150
to solve the math problems.

629
00:31:01,150 --> 00:31:03,500
The prompt itself
looks pretty long,

630
00:31:03,500 --> 00:31:06,940
but basically there are
two main components.

631
00:31:06,940 --> 00:31:09,850
The first one is this
[? text ?] trajectory,

632
00:31:09,850 --> 00:31:13,890
including the instructions we
have explored in the past steps,

633
00:31:13,890 --> 00:31:16,270
and with their
corresponding accuracies.

634
00:31:16,270 --> 00:31:19,740
And in practice, we will sort
them in the ascending order

635
00:31:19,740 --> 00:31:21,910
and we can remove
those older ones

636
00:31:21,910 --> 00:31:27,120
if the context is not enough to
put all of them in the history.

637
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:28,920
And we also provide
this exemplars

638
00:31:28,920 --> 00:31:31,260
to show what's the task
we want to optimize.

639
00:31:31,260 --> 00:31:34,300
And basically here, for
example, for math problems

640
00:31:34,300 --> 00:31:38,440
we only need to include this QA
pairs and with the final answer.

641
00:31:38,440 --> 00:31:40,350
So again, there's
no need to annotate

642
00:31:40,350 --> 00:31:44,100
any train-of-thought or
any explanations of how

643
00:31:44,100 --> 00:31:45,365
we can derive this solution.

644
00:31:49,110 --> 00:31:51,690
Then for the results,
basically, again we

645
00:31:51,690 --> 00:31:53,940
are testing all
these instructions

646
00:31:53,940 --> 00:31:56,290
on pre-trained only
large language models.

647
00:31:56,290 --> 00:31:58,690
And we use prompt
tool at that time.

648
00:31:58,690 --> 00:32:02,470
And we also try using different
models as optimizers including

649
00:32:02,470 --> 00:32:06,370
PaLM model and the GPT model.

650
00:32:06,370 --> 00:32:09,040
So for all these
approaches, we are

651
00:32:09,040 --> 00:32:12,010
starting from the initial
instruction with let's solve

652
00:32:12,010 --> 00:32:15,880
the problem, which achieves
an accuracy of around 61%

653
00:32:15,880 --> 00:32:17,690
for this GSM8K task.

654
00:32:17,690 --> 00:32:20,540
And this is a pretty
general purpose instruction.

655
00:32:20,540 --> 00:32:23,747
It is around 10% lower
than the instruction

656
00:32:23,747 --> 00:32:24,705
let's see step by step.

657
00:32:28,030 --> 00:32:30,610
And if we look at the
best LLM generative prompt

658
00:32:30,610 --> 00:32:34,270
among this list, we can see that
actually this instruction takes

659
00:32:34,270 --> 00:32:37,460
a deep breath and work on
this problem step by step.

660
00:32:37,460 --> 00:32:40,870
Achieves around 8% like low--

661
00:32:40,870 --> 00:32:43,910
like higher than this, let's
think, step by step instruction.

662
00:32:43,910 --> 00:32:47,410
And actually if you compare this
performance to few-shot CoT,

663
00:32:47,410 --> 00:32:51,250
it matches the performance
reported in the PaLM 2 paper,

664
00:32:51,250 --> 00:32:54,610
where the train-of-thought
examplars are written by humans.

665
00:32:54,610 --> 00:32:58,010
So there are two things
we can think about here.

666
00:32:58,010 --> 00:33:01,140
One is that basically here
where I seeing another way

667
00:33:01,140 --> 00:33:04,980
to basically achieve very
competitive performance

668
00:33:04,980 --> 00:33:06,810
to few-shot CoT, we
saw that manually

669
00:33:06,810 --> 00:33:08,820
writing examples ourselves.

670
00:33:08,820 --> 00:33:13,200
Another perspective is that
with this type of LLM based

671
00:33:13,200 --> 00:33:17,190
prompt optimization, it
doesn't only save us time to

672
00:33:17,190 --> 00:33:20,910
so that we do not need to spend
so much time to manually tune

673
00:33:20,910 --> 00:33:22,210
the prompts ourselves.

674
00:33:22,210 --> 00:33:24,460
But actually, when it
comes up with new prompts,

675
00:33:24,460 --> 00:33:28,270
it also provides sometimes
surprising different angles.

676
00:33:28,270 --> 00:33:30,730
Like I can assume
that, for example,

677
00:33:30,730 --> 00:33:33,480
like [INAUDIBLE]
take a deep breath.

678
00:33:33,480 --> 00:33:39,190
At least for me, if I'm trying
to revise the prompt by myself,

679
00:33:39,190 --> 00:33:43,570
I won't feel that this phrase
will be so, so helpful,

680
00:33:43,570 --> 00:33:47,370
but empirically, the large
model with this optimization

681
00:33:47,370 --> 00:33:50,460
loop actually it
can somehow come up

682
00:33:50,460 --> 00:33:53,570
with this instruction that fits
pretty well for this model.

683
00:33:57,210 --> 00:33:59,170
And note that, in
this [? text ?]

684
00:33:59,170 --> 00:34:01,850
LLM-based optimization
process, actually,

685
00:34:01,850 --> 00:34:04,030
this is not a real
optimization in the sense

686
00:34:04,030 --> 00:34:06,530
that we do not have
any training here.

687
00:34:06,530 --> 00:34:09,164
It is completely prompting only.

688
00:34:09,164 --> 00:34:13,179
So there's no guarantee that the
model can just keep coming up

689
00:34:13,179 --> 00:34:15,325
with better and better prompts.

690
00:34:15,325 --> 00:34:19,255
But in practice, we find that,
actually, this optimization

691
00:34:19,255 --> 00:34:22,969
curve holds for
different models.

692
00:34:22,969 --> 00:34:27,010
So we can see that basically
with more optimization

693
00:34:27,010 --> 00:34:30,710
steps, which is basically this
iterative prompting steps,

694
00:34:30,710 --> 00:34:33,830
the accuracy increases with
more steps and then plateaus.

695
00:34:33,830 --> 00:34:37,975
So it looks pretty like similar
to the traditional optimization

696
00:34:37,975 --> 00:34:38,475
graphs.

697
00:34:41,900 --> 00:34:42,400
OK.

698
00:34:42,400 --> 00:34:45,565
Now let's go back
to Chain-of-Thought.

699
00:34:45,565 --> 00:34:48,170
So from the methodology
perspective,

700
00:34:48,170 --> 00:34:51,409
what does Chain-of-Thought
really bring into our reasoning?

701
00:34:51,409 --> 00:34:54,280
Why is it so effective?

702
00:34:54,280 --> 00:34:56,719
If we think about it,
Chain-of-Thought prompting

703
00:34:56,719 --> 00:35:00,230
is basically giving
us a way to perform

704
00:35:00,230 --> 00:35:03,560
this variable computation
of the process, which

705
00:35:03,560 --> 00:35:05,825
will lead the number
of tokens used

706
00:35:05,825 --> 00:35:09,830
for problem will be adapted to
task of different difficulty

707
00:35:09,830 --> 00:35:11,030
levels.

708
00:35:11,030 --> 00:35:14,720
So if we think about standard
prompting, basically,

709
00:35:14,720 --> 00:35:18,330
for the same task, if the
answer learns are the same,

710
00:35:18,330 --> 00:35:20,570
then basically, if we just
provide the final answer

711
00:35:20,570 --> 00:35:23,150
directly, it basically means
that we are forcing the model

712
00:35:23,150 --> 00:35:26,180
to use the same amount of time,
same amount of inference time

713
00:35:26,180 --> 00:35:30,350
budget to solve each problem,
regardless of its difficulty.

714
00:35:30,350 --> 00:35:32,220
But with this, like
a Chain-of-Thought,

715
00:35:32,220 --> 00:35:35,190
basically, it shows that
for more complex questions,

716
00:35:35,190 --> 00:35:38,830
because it also require more
reasoning steps to solve

717
00:35:38,830 --> 00:35:40,330
the problem, like
for math problems,

718
00:35:40,330 --> 00:35:42,600
it might require more
calculation steps.

719
00:35:42,600 --> 00:35:44,790
So basically, with this
Chain-of-Thought idea,

720
00:35:44,790 --> 00:35:48,710
it allows the model to
also perform more reasoning

721
00:35:48,710 --> 00:35:52,340
steps in the token space
so that it can better

722
00:35:52,340 --> 00:35:54,750
solve the task problems.

723
00:35:54,750 --> 00:35:56,170
And in the
Chain-of-Thought paper,

724
00:35:56,170 --> 00:36:01,300
they also show that even if
the difficulty of the problems

725
00:36:01,300 --> 00:36:04,700
is in the exemplars are
pretty simple math problems,

726
00:36:04,700 --> 00:36:06,460
but actually, the
same problem can also

727
00:36:06,460 --> 00:36:09,340
generalize to test problems
that require much more reasoning

728
00:36:09,340 --> 00:36:09,840
steps.

729
00:36:13,030 --> 00:36:16,160
Then, if we think about the
high level reasoning strategies,

730
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:18,250
Chain-of-Thought also
incorporates a lot

731
00:36:18,250 --> 00:36:22,660
of reasoning process that are
very important for us humans

732
00:36:22,660 --> 00:36:27,647
to reason about difficult task
such as decomposition, planning,

733
00:36:27,647 --> 00:36:28,230
and et cetera.

734
00:36:31,120 --> 00:36:33,920
So if you want to further
improve the model performance,

735
00:36:33,920 --> 00:36:35,380
and especially if
we know what are

736
00:36:35,380 --> 00:36:37,870
some good guidelines on
the reasoning strategies

737
00:36:37,870 --> 00:36:40,490
we need to use for
a specific task,

738
00:36:40,490 --> 00:36:42,550
we can also explicitly
instruct the LLM

739
00:36:42,550 --> 00:36:45,420
with the desired reasoning
strategies for problem solving.

740
00:36:48,190 --> 00:36:52,270
So one idea to further push
this decomposition strategy

741
00:36:52,270 --> 00:36:54,720
is called least-to-most
prompting.

742
00:36:54,720 --> 00:36:57,870
And the goal of this
approach is to achieve

743
00:36:57,870 --> 00:37:00,950
easy-to-hard generalization
by explicitly telling

744
00:37:00,950 --> 00:37:03,800
the model what's the best
practice to decompose

745
00:37:03,800 --> 00:37:05,810
the original problem.

746
00:37:05,810 --> 00:37:07,940
So in the slides, I'm
showing this example

747
00:37:07,940 --> 00:37:10,330
for solving math problems.

748
00:37:10,330 --> 00:37:13,370
And basically, in this original
least-to-most prompting paper

749
00:37:13,370 --> 00:37:15,230
from my colleagues
at Google DeepMind,

750
00:37:15,230 --> 00:37:17,905
it includes two stages.

751
00:37:17,905 --> 00:37:21,150
So in the first stage, it
performs the problem reduction.

752
00:37:21,150 --> 00:37:24,440
Like given a math problem,
it first reduces the problems

753
00:37:24,440 --> 00:37:26,210
into simpler subproblems.

754
00:37:26,210 --> 00:37:29,570
So each sub math problem
can be easier to solve,

755
00:37:29,570 --> 00:37:32,240
requires fewer
calculation steps.

756
00:37:32,240 --> 00:37:34,940
Then given this
subquestions the model

757
00:37:34,940 --> 00:37:39,050
will be able to sequentially
solve these subproblems

758
00:37:39,050 --> 00:37:41,530
and combine the solutions to
solve the original problem.

759
00:37:44,240 --> 00:37:47,780
The most impressive results from
this work is that, actually,

760
00:37:47,780 --> 00:37:50,425
with this least-to-most
prompting design,

761
00:37:50,425 --> 00:37:54,100
it shows for the first time
that we do not need to design

762
00:37:54,100 --> 00:37:57,530
a [? neural-symbolic ?]
frameworks or requiring any

763
00:37:57,530 --> 00:37:59,500
program execution.

764
00:37:59,500 --> 00:38:03,910
But it can still solve this
compositional generalization

765
00:38:03,910 --> 00:38:07,135
benchmarks with nearly
perfect accuracy.

766
00:38:07,135 --> 00:38:11,510
So the results show in their
paper is on this SCAN benchmark.

767
00:38:11,510 --> 00:38:15,250
So SCAN is a synthetic
data about translating

768
00:38:15,250 --> 00:38:17,680
programmatically
generated natural language

769
00:38:17,680 --> 00:38:20,350
commands into action sequences.

770
00:38:20,350 --> 00:38:22,870
The most challenging
part of this benchmark

771
00:38:22,870 --> 00:38:25,045
is about this length split.

772
00:38:25,045 --> 00:38:27,380
Basically, in their
[? text ?] splits,

773
00:38:27,380 --> 00:38:29,620
all the action
sequences are longer

774
00:38:29,620 --> 00:38:31,010
than the training samples.

775
00:38:31,010 --> 00:38:32,680
So this makes it
very challenging

776
00:38:32,680 --> 00:38:34,900
for the traditional like
sequence-to-sequence

777
00:38:34,900 --> 00:38:39,280
learning if we directly do this
training on their training set.

778
00:38:39,280 --> 00:38:41,530
And then on this, length
split, the test accuracy

779
00:38:41,530 --> 00:38:44,500
will be no more
than 25% unless we

780
00:38:44,500 --> 00:38:47,290
apply some neural-symbolic
techniques.

781
00:38:47,290 --> 00:38:49,950
But then basically, with
least-to-most prompting,

782
00:38:49,950 --> 00:38:53,570
they show that if they
use the latest models

783
00:38:53,570 --> 00:38:57,810
at that time, which is
the code-davinci-002,

784
00:38:57,810 --> 00:39:00,900
they are able to achieve
nearly perfect test accuracy.

785
00:39:00,900 --> 00:39:02,870
And at the same
time, they only need

786
00:39:02,870 --> 00:39:08,040
to use 0.1% of the training
samples as exemplars.

787
00:39:08,040 --> 00:39:12,950
So it is both test
efficient or data efficient

788
00:39:12,950 --> 00:39:15,680
to adapt to new tasks
while also the performance

789
00:39:15,680 --> 00:39:17,120
is very competitive.

790
00:39:17,120 --> 00:39:21,210
Learning our follow up work
published at ICLR last year,

791
00:39:21,210 --> 00:39:24,050
we further show that we can
extend this least-to-most

792
00:39:24,050 --> 00:39:27,590
prompting idea to
solve more complicated

793
00:39:27,590 --> 00:39:31,280
compositional generalization
in the case of translating text

794
00:39:31,280 --> 00:39:34,220
to more realistic
programming languages.

795
00:39:34,220 --> 00:39:36,530
Like here on the slides,
I'm showing this example

796
00:39:36,530 --> 00:39:40,940
of translating natural language
questions to SPARQL queries.

797
00:39:40,940 --> 00:39:44,960
So the main challenge here
is that for more real world

798
00:39:44,960 --> 00:39:47,650
languages, including natural
language or programming

799
00:39:47,650 --> 00:39:50,740
languages, there are more
complicated grammar rules

800
00:39:50,740 --> 00:39:54,400
and also the vocabulary
size is larger.

801
00:39:54,400 --> 00:39:57,190
This means that,
especially, if we do not

802
00:39:57,190 --> 00:39:59,840
have a model with a
super long context,

803
00:39:59,840 --> 00:40:02,275
then a single prompt
will be no longer

804
00:40:02,275 --> 00:40:06,940
enough to cover all the grammar
rules needed to solve the task.

805
00:40:06,940 --> 00:40:09,140
So actually, with
this challenge,

806
00:40:09,140 --> 00:40:12,550
it shows one benefit
of decomposition

807
00:40:12,550 --> 00:40:15,100
by allowing the model
to use different prompts

808
00:40:15,100 --> 00:40:16,820
for different reasoning stages.

809
00:40:16,820 --> 00:40:20,350
It also enables the model
to use customized prompts

810
00:40:20,350 --> 00:40:23,200
for each subproblem
or each subtask

811
00:40:23,200 --> 00:40:26,005
in the context of
agent development.

812
00:40:26,005 --> 00:40:29,320
So here is an overview of our
dynamic least-to-most prompting

813
00:40:29,320 --> 00:40:30,020
approach.

814
00:40:30,020 --> 00:40:32,950
So some details do not
matter too much here like how

815
00:40:32,950 --> 00:40:34,370
we do the decomposition.

816
00:40:34,370 --> 00:40:36,250
But the [? menu ?]
stage we add is

817
00:40:36,250 --> 00:40:39,520
this so-called dynamic
selection of exemplars

818
00:40:39,520 --> 00:40:40,850
for each subproblem.

819
00:40:40,850 --> 00:40:45,350
So basically here, for each
of test, like a sentence,

820
00:40:45,350 --> 00:40:48,140
we can discover those
example questions

821
00:40:48,140 --> 00:40:50,480
with the most relevant
grammar rules.

822
00:40:50,480 --> 00:40:53,580
So basically, we can assure that
for each part of the sentence,

823
00:40:53,580 --> 00:40:57,680
we can always retrieve for
the most useful grammar rules

824
00:40:57,680 --> 00:41:00,230
to solve the task so that the
model can have enough context

825
00:41:00,230 --> 00:41:01,315
to solve the problem.

826
00:41:04,505 --> 00:41:08,390
So all these compositional
[? three ?] [? base ?] question

827
00:41:08,390 --> 00:41:10,875
benchmark, the CFQ data set.

828
00:41:10,875 --> 00:41:13,310
Again, we show that, actually,
our dynamic least-to-most

829
00:41:13,310 --> 00:41:16,790
prompting approach outperforms
all these baselines, which

830
00:41:16,790 --> 00:41:21,140
where we need to train a model
on the complete training set.

831
00:41:21,140 --> 00:41:24,590
And again, if we compare this
dynamic least-to-most prompting

832
00:41:24,590 --> 00:41:27,660
with Chain-of-Thought prompting
and other prompting approaches,

833
00:41:27,660 --> 00:41:31,160
it also scales better when
we increase this examplar

834
00:41:31,160 --> 00:41:35,990
[? problem ?] to generate
these few-shot [? examplars. ?]

835
00:41:35,990 --> 00:41:39,140
So far, we have talked about
how we can instruct the model

836
00:41:39,140 --> 00:41:43,430
to solve the problem in some
like with some reasoning

837
00:41:43,430 --> 00:41:47,290
strategies indicated by
the few-shot examplars.

838
00:41:47,290 --> 00:41:50,110
But if we think about
it, we can assume

839
00:41:50,110 --> 00:41:52,280
that for different
reasoning tasks,

840
00:41:52,280 --> 00:41:55,190
they can require very
different reasoning structures.

841
00:41:55,190 --> 00:41:56,800
Like there will
be different ways

842
00:41:56,800 --> 00:42:00,700
to decompose the task
and plan for each stage.

843
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:02,170
At the same time,
we might not want

844
00:42:02,170 --> 00:42:06,605
to annotate all these best
practices for each type of task.

845
00:42:06,605 --> 00:42:09,700
Again, we don't want to do this
manual labeling of exemplars

846
00:42:09,700 --> 00:42:12,340
for every single problem.

847
00:42:12,340 --> 00:42:16,490
So in our self-discover work, we
published at NeurIPS last year,

848
00:42:16,490 --> 00:42:19,570
we basically instruct
the model to compose

849
00:42:19,570 --> 00:42:22,480
task-specific reasoning
structures without manually

850
00:42:22,480 --> 00:42:24,070
written demonstrations.

851
00:42:24,070 --> 00:42:26,560
So in practice, how we do
it is that basically, we

852
00:42:26,560 --> 00:42:30,100
will list a kind of
[INAUDIBLE] of best practice

853
00:42:30,100 --> 00:42:31,870
of reasoning
strategies, including

854
00:42:31,870 --> 00:42:34,610
like a Chain-of-Thought
reasoning, doing decomposition,

855
00:42:34,610 --> 00:42:36,460
doing some
self-reflection, et cetera,

856
00:42:36,460 --> 00:42:38,720
then given a
different, a new task,

857
00:42:38,720 --> 00:42:43,850
the model will think about how
it can utilize the reasoning

858
00:42:43,850 --> 00:42:46,670
strategies that are best
suited for the current task

859
00:42:46,670 --> 00:42:48,170
and compose the
reasoning structure.

860
00:42:48,170 --> 00:42:49,690
Follow it to solve the problem.

861
00:42:52,820 --> 00:42:56,000
And for evaluation, we also show
that with this self-discovery

862
00:42:56,000 --> 00:42:58,940
methodology, it outperforms
Chain-of-Thought prompting

863
00:42:58,940 --> 00:43:02,760
and also, other baselines
on different benchmarks,

864
00:43:02,760 --> 00:43:05,450
including BIG-Bench-Hard
reasoning task,

865
00:43:05,450 --> 00:43:08,030
[? MATH ?] benchmark, and
also some other benchmarks

866
00:43:08,030 --> 00:43:13,010
for [? socio-agent ?] reasoning.

867
00:43:13,010 --> 00:43:16,578
So to summarize, in the
first part I talked about,

868
00:43:16,578 --> 00:43:18,620
I first talked about
Chain-of-Thought generation.

869
00:43:18,620 --> 00:43:21,710
And the fundamental idea
of allowing the model

870
00:43:21,710 --> 00:43:25,890
to perform variable computation
to trigger the thought process.

871
00:43:25,890 --> 00:43:28,460
And this thought process
can adapt to tasks

872
00:43:28,460 --> 00:43:30,025
of different difficulty levels.

873
00:43:32,840 --> 00:43:34,340
And we talked about
how to improve

874
00:43:34,340 --> 00:43:37,630
the Chain-of-Thought
performance at inference time.

875
00:43:37,630 --> 00:43:39,380
So in the original
Chain-of-Thought paper,

876
00:43:39,380 --> 00:43:41,280
we talk about the
few-shot prompting idea

877
00:43:41,280 --> 00:43:43,290
with labeling of thoughts.

878
00:43:43,290 --> 00:43:45,220
And we talked about
instruction prompting

879
00:43:45,220 --> 00:43:48,600
to trigger this
Chain-of-Thought generation.

880
00:43:48,600 --> 00:43:51,210
And also, we talked about how
we can instruct a large language

881
00:43:51,210 --> 00:43:53,280
model to automate
the prompt design

882
00:43:53,280 --> 00:43:55,660
and discover better
problems by themselves.

883
00:43:58,740 --> 00:44:01,140
So notice that
this best practice

884
00:44:01,140 --> 00:44:03,180
of interacting with
large language models

885
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:04,930
can evolve over time.

886
00:44:04,930 --> 00:44:07,860
Like for example, recently with
this [? post-trained ?] large

887
00:44:07,860 --> 00:44:11,220
language models, you do not
have to put exemplars for every

888
00:44:11,220 --> 00:44:14,280
single task for the model
to learn how to do it.

889
00:44:14,280 --> 00:44:18,180
And for some models, for
example, it is better for you

890
00:44:18,180 --> 00:44:21,180
to just provide very clear
guidelines and instructions

891
00:44:21,180 --> 00:44:22,330
on what you want.

892
00:44:22,330 --> 00:44:25,510
And then hope the model can
just follow your instructions.

893
00:44:25,510 --> 00:44:28,990
And some models prefer those
like an interactive discussion

894
00:44:28,990 --> 00:44:31,770
and some models might prefer
just a very long and detailed

895
00:44:31,770 --> 00:44:34,110
instruction at once.

896
00:44:34,110 --> 00:44:36,120
But the principles
of how to discover

897
00:44:36,120 --> 00:44:39,560
good prompting strategies for
reasoning still hold here.

898
00:44:39,560 --> 00:44:42,710
Like two very
important criteria is

899
00:44:42,710 --> 00:44:46,220
that we want this approach
to encourage the model

900
00:44:46,220 --> 00:44:47,990
to generate longer
Chain-of-Thought

901
00:44:47,990 --> 00:44:49,230
for complex tasks.

902
00:44:49,230 --> 00:44:53,570
We want it to scale with
different task difficulty.

903
00:44:53,570 --> 00:44:55,700
And also, we need this
prompting technique

904
00:44:55,700 --> 00:44:58,370
to be able to support
reasoning strategies required

905
00:44:58,370 --> 00:44:59,900
for the task.

906
00:44:59,900 --> 00:45:01,700
So now, let's start
the second part

907
00:45:01,700 --> 00:45:03,680
about perform
search and selection

908
00:45:03,680 --> 00:45:06,350
from multiple candidates
and increasing the width

909
00:45:06,350 --> 00:45:07,885
to explore the solution space.

910
00:45:10,400 --> 00:45:15,090
So after we talk about the first
part, what is missing so far?

911
00:45:15,090 --> 00:45:16,970
Yeah, I guess this
question is not

912
00:45:16,970 --> 00:45:20,400
so hard since I already showed
the outline of this slides.

913
00:45:20,400 --> 00:45:23,575
So basically, from
first part, we

914
00:45:23,575 --> 00:45:25,700
can see that we should not
limit the large language

915
00:45:25,700 --> 00:45:29,450
model to generate only
one solution per problem.

916
00:45:29,450 --> 00:45:33,470
Because the model can just
have some mistakes in its

917
00:45:33,470 --> 00:45:36,600
[? single-pass ?] solution.

918
00:45:36,600 --> 00:45:38,160
Instead, we should
allow the model

919
00:45:38,160 --> 00:45:40,200
to explore multiple
branches, which

920
00:45:40,200 --> 00:45:43,260
will allow the large language
model to recover from mistakes

921
00:45:43,260 --> 00:45:45,150
in a single generation.

922
00:45:45,150 --> 00:45:47,910
And there are two ways
to basically increase

923
00:45:47,910 --> 00:45:50,850
the width of this
solution exploration.

924
00:45:50,850 --> 00:45:53,040
One is that we can
generate multiple candidate

925
00:45:53,040 --> 00:45:55,500
solutions per problem.

926
00:45:55,500 --> 00:45:57,480
Another is that,
at each step, we

927
00:45:57,480 --> 00:46:00,240
can allow the model to generate
multiple potential next

928
00:46:00,240 --> 00:46:03,120
reasoning steps.

929
00:46:03,120 --> 00:46:08,040
It is not that hard to think
that with this multiple sample

930
00:46:08,040 --> 00:46:10,770
generation, then the upper
bound of the performance

931
00:46:10,770 --> 00:46:12,570
will be definitely
higher because we

932
00:46:12,570 --> 00:46:16,390
allow the model to
select an alternative,

933
00:46:16,390 --> 00:46:18,570
probably better solution.

934
00:46:18,570 --> 00:46:22,290
But the fundamental challenge
for approaches along this line

935
00:46:22,290 --> 00:46:25,170
is that how to select
the best response

936
00:46:25,170 --> 00:46:28,050
from multiple candidates.

937
00:46:28,050 --> 00:46:29,680
Because in the real
world use case,

938
00:46:29,680 --> 00:46:32,880
we do not have an Oracle
Scholar at inference time.

939
00:46:32,880 --> 00:46:35,580
So surely, for example,
in the chat interface,

940
00:46:35,580 --> 00:46:37,190
we can just have
the model generate

941
00:46:37,190 --> 00:46:40,740
multiple solutions and the user
can pick whatever they want.

942
00:46:40,740 --> 00:46:44,710
Then this is kind of a best
of [? selection ?] case.

943
00:46:44,710 --> 00:46:47,540
But it will be more ideal
if the model can just

944
00:46:47,540 --> 00:46:50,120
do this kind of
process internally

945
00:46:50,120 --> 00:46:54,650
so that we do not have to
review like tens of solutions

946
00:46:54,650 --> 00:46:56,605
by ourselves to
select what we want.

947
00:46:59,510 --> 00:47:02,190
So along the line
of these ideas,

948
00:47:02,190 --> 00:47:05,510
I want to first talk about
this self-consistency work

949
00:47:05,510 --> 00:47:08,105
from my colleagues
at Google DeepMind.

950
00:47:08,105 --> 00:47:11,460
So if we look at
this idea nowadays,

951
00:47:11,460 --> 00:47:15,000
we will find that this is
an incredibly simple idea.

952
00:47:15,000 --> 00:47:16,650
But it is actually
very effective.

953
00:47:16,650 --> 00:47:22,160
And it actually can
improve performance a lot

954
00:47:22,160 --> 00:47:25,280
with all the different models.

955
00:47:25,280 --> 00:47:28,165
So let's say we want to
also solve a math problem.

956
00:47:28,165 --> 00:47:29,540
And this time, we
allow the model

957
00:47:29,540 --> 00:47:32,150
to generate multiple responses.

958
00:47:32,150 --> 00:47:36,010
So basically, after we
have all these responses,

959
00:47:36,010 --> 00:47:38,650
we still do not know
which one is accurate.

960
00:47:38,650 --> 00:47:40,680
But here, we have
this consistency

961
00:47:40,680 --> 00:47:42,580
based selection criterion.

962
00:47:42,580 --> 00:47:45,360
So basically, if let's
say for this math problem

963
00:47:45,360 --> 00:47:48,190
with a single number
as the final answer,

964
00:47:48,190 --> 00:47:50,110
we will look at these
three solutions.

965
00:47:50,110 --> 00:47:54,640
Two of them have the answer 18
and of them have the answer 26.

966
00:47:54,640 --> 00:47:57,910
So basically 18 is the
most consistent answer.

967
00:47:57,910 --> 00:48:00,750
So we pick the response
corresponding to this.

968
00:48:00,750 --> 00:48:05,010
Like most frequently
appeared answer.

969
00:48:05,010 --> 00:48:07,200
And note that here,
one important thing

970
00:48:07,200 --> 00:48:10,630
is that this selection is only
based on the final answer.

971
00:48:10,630 --> 00:48:12,550
It is not based on
the reasoning process.

972
00:48:12,550 --> 00:48:14,400
The model can come
up with whatever

973
00:48:14,400 --> 00:48:15,970
reasoning process it wants.

974
00:48:15,970 --> 00:48:18,340
So as long as they lead
to the same final answer,

975
00:48:18,340 --> 00:48:21,240
we consider them to be all good.

976
00:48:21,240 --> 00:48:22,930
This is a pretty simple idea.

977
00:48:22,930 --> 00:48:25,470
But actually, it
boosts the performance

978
00:48:25,470 --> 00:48:27,820
across different
models and benchmarks.

979
00:48:27,820 --> 00:48:31,000
And here, in their
paper, they also--

980
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:34,310
I evaluate all the models
available at that time,

981
00:48:34,310 --> 00:48:36,440
and especially on math problems.

982
00:48:36,440 --> 00:48:39,880
They show very significant
performance improvement.

983
00:48:39,880 --> 00:48:42,400
Now let's talk about
this scaling effect

984
00:48:42,400 --> 00:48:46,465
again with the
self-consistency approach.

985
00:48:46,465 --> 00:48:49,570
So for the baseline
here in the curves,

986
00:48:49,570 --> 00:48:52,790
they compare with
sample-and-rank baseline.

987
00:48:52,790 --> 00:48:55,280
This is actually a
pretty natural baseline,

988
00:48:55,280 --> 00:48:58,720
which says that we can select
the response with the highest

989
00:48:58,720 --> 00:49:00,315
log probability.

990
00:49:00,315 --> 00:49:04,570
So then here, the assumption is
that if the model gives higher

991
00:49:04,570 --> 00:49:06,110
probability to the
current response,

992
00:49:06,110 --> 00:49:08,770
it should be more
confident about it.

993
00:49:08,770 --> 00:49:10,880
But actually, if we
look at these curves,

994
00:49:10,880 --> 00:49:14,650
we can see that self-consistency
performance scales much better

995
00:49:14,650 --> 00:49:16,460
than probability-based ranking.

996
00:49:16,460 --> 00:49:21,170
So it's more and more responses,
like up to 40 responses.

997
00:49:21,170 --> 00:49:23,750
Self-consistency performance
is still improving.

998
00:49:23,750 --> 00:49:25,660
But these
sample-and-rank baseline

999
00:49:25,660 --> 00:49:33,120
has already stopped improving
around 10 responses already.

1000
00:49:33,120 --> 00:49:34,780
But there is an exception.

1001
00:49:34,780 --> 00:49:36,690
So the exception is
that, if we train

1002
00:49:36,690 --> 00:49:39,990
the model to be a very good
verifier for the task we care

1003
00:49:39,990 --> 00:49:44,580
about, we might be able
to outperform this scaling

1004
00:49:44,580 --> 00:49:46,420
curve with self-consistency.

1005
00:49:46,420 --> 00:49:48,870
And we will talk about it later.

1006
00:49:48,870 --> 00:49:51,090
Another very
important factor that

1007
00:49:51,090 --> 00:49:53,550
contributes to
self-consistency success

1008
00:49:53,550 --> 00:49:59,220
is that we need the model to
generate very diverse responses

1009
00:49:59,220 --> 00:50:02,820
to perform this
consistency-based selection.

1010
00:50:02,820 --> 00:50:05,820
So here, basically
in this results,

1011
00:50:05,820 --> 00:50:08,530
they also compare with
different baselines.

1012
00:50:08,530 --> 00:50:12,150
So the beam search is actually
a very classic search algorithm

1013
00:50:12,150 --> 00:50:16,930
like in the NLP literature
before the [? LLM ?] error.

1014
00:50:16,930 --> 00:50:20,100
So basically, the idea is
that at each decoding step,

1015
00:50:20,100 --> 00:50:22,890
we will keep the top
[? k ?] like tokens

1016
00:50:22,890 --> 00:50:24,430
with the highest probabilities.

1017
00:50:24,430 --> 00:50:28,840
Then in the end, we will use
a sentence with the highest

1018
00:50:28,840 --> 00:50:31,960
overall probability.

1019
00:50:31,960 --> 00:50:36,640
And then they also compare with
this [? sample ?] baselines,

1020
00:50:36,640 --> 00:50:41,698
where they apply greedy decoding
among different variants of how

1021
00:50:41,698 --> 00:50:42,740
you formulate the prompt.

1022
00:50:42,740 --> 00:50:44,740
Because earlier we
mentioned that the LLMs are

1023
00:50:44,740 --> 00:50:46,460
sensitive to different prompts.

1024
00:50:46,460 --> 00:50:49,285
Also, they compare the
performance to different ways

1025
00:50:49,285 --> 00:50:54,250
to permute the exemplars
orders in the prompt.

1026
00:50:54,250 --> 00:50:56,560
So again, they show
that self-consistency

1027
00:50:56,560 --> 00:51:00,745
using sampling scales
better with more samples.

1028
00:51:00,745 --> 00:51:05,530
So the important factor here is
that basically for the sampling

1029
00:51:05,530 --> 00:51:09,290
method, it needs to ensure that
the responses are very diverse.

1030
00:51:09,290 --> 00:51:13,550
So we can do this sampling
with a high temperature.

1031
00:51:13,550 --> 00:51:16,660
Or we can perform
nucleus sampling

1032
00:51:16,660 --> 00:51:18,880
by setting the parameters
in the right way

1033
00:51:18,880 --> 00:51:22,030
to enforce this
diversity, et cetera.

1034
00:51:22,030 --> 00:51:25,520
One analysis from this work is
actually pretty interesting.

1035
00:51:25,520 --> 00:51:28,350
It is related to
model calibration.

1036
00:51:28,350 --> 00:51:32,070
So this is about
actually how does

1037
00:51:32,070 --> 00:51:36,190
this consistency-based selection
improve the model performance.

1038
00:51:36,190 --> 00:51:41,160
What does it mean when the
model, for a given task,

1039
00:51:41,160 --> 00:51:44,850
the model can come up with
a more consistent response?

1040
00:51:44,850 --> 00:51:48,000
So here, they show
this curve to show

1041
00:51:48,000 --> 00:51:52,250
the correlation between the
accuracy and consistency.

1042
00:51:52,250 --> 00:51:56,220
So here, we can see
that if it follows,

1043
00:51:56,220 --> 00:51:58,260
sample responses,
if more of them

1044
00:51:58,260 --> 00:52:00,240
lead to the same
final answer, this

1045
00:52:00,240 --> 00:52:02,950
means that this consistency
percentage is higher.

1046
00:52:02,950 --> 00:52:06,850
Then there are two conclusions
we can see from here.

1047
00:52:06,850 --> 00:52:08,490
One is that this
large language model

1048
00:52:08,490 --> 00:52:11,680
is more certain with its
final predicted conclusion

1049
00:52:11,680 --> 00:52:14,470
even if they are coming up
with the same conclusion

1050
00:52:14,470 --> 00:52:16,530
with different
reasoning process.

1051
00:52:16,530 --> 00:52:19,530
And empirically, we see that
this aggregated solution

1052
00:52:19,530 --> 00:52:22,570
for this, more consistent
[? text ?] responses,

1053
00:52:22,570 --> 00:52:24,640
can be more likely
to be accurate.

1054
00:52:24,640 --> 00:52:27,670
The final solution can
just be the right one

1055
00:52:27,670 --> 00:52:29,630
for the original test problem.

1056
00:52:29,630 --> 00:52:32,590
So this can also explain
why this consistency can

1057
00:52:32,590 --> 00:52:34,750
be a very simple,
but very powerful

1058
00:52:34,750 --> 00:52:38,005
criterion to select
the final solution.

1059
00:52:38,005 --> 00:52:39,640
So in the
self-consistency paper,

1060
00:52:39,640 --> 00:52:43,510
they demonstrate this approach
on those test-based reasoning

1061
00:52:43,510 --> 00:52:44,420
problems.

1062
00:52:44,420 --> 00:52:47,440
And actually, this
consistency-based selection

1063
00:52:47,440 --> 00:52:51,670
approach is also very
powerful for code generation.

1064
00:52:51,670 --> 00:52:57,160
And in our AlphaCode
work at Google DeepMind,

1065
00:52:57,160 --> 00:53:00,890
this AlphaCode system itself
is a pretty complicated one.

1066
00:53:00,890 --> 00:53:04,190
It includes the model training
and inference time techniques.

1067
00:53:04,190 --> 00:53:09,370
But here, I will mainly focus
on this inference stage called

1068
00:53:09,370 --> 00:53:11,120
filtering and clustering.

1069
00:53:11,120 --> 00:53:14,440
And especially, this
clustering is basically tries

1070
00:53:14,440 --> 00:53:19,300
to do this like a code selection
based on the consistency

1071
00:53:19,300 --> 00:53:22,830
[? of ?] execution results.

1072
00:53:22,830 --> 00:53:26,350
So let's first briefly
discuss about the background,

1073
00:53:26,350 --> 00:53:29,500
the problem setting of
competitive programming.

1074
00:53:29,500 --> 00:53:33,180
So basically, in a competitive
programming problem,

1075
00:53:33,180 --> 00:53:34,860
in the problem
description, it will

1076
00:53:34,860 --> 00:53:37,410
include a very long
and complicated task

1077
00:53:37,410 --> 00:53:39,010
to tell about the
background story,

1078
00:53:39,010 --> 00:53:41,175
about what we want
for the problem

1079
00:53:41,175 --> 00:53:42,820
for the corresponding code.

1080
00:53:42,820 --> 00:53:45,360
And then also, it includes
a few input-output pairs

1081
00:53:45,360 --> 00:53:48,210
as test cases.

1082
00:53:48,210 --> 00:53:50,920
Then for the solution to
be considered correct,

1083
00:53:50,920 --> 00:53:54,030
the generated code needs
to pass both the given test

1084
00:53:54,030 --> 00:53:55,690
cases in the prompt.

1085
00:53:55,690 --> 00:53:58,930
And also, usually, we will
have some held-out test cases.

1086
00:53:58,930 --> 00:54:02,610
And these test cases are
typically of larger scale

1087
00:54:02,610 --> 00:54:07,510
and they will be more difficult
than this example test cases.

1088
00:54:07,510 --> 00:54:09,460
And they will cover
some corner cases.

1089
00:54:09,460 --> 00:54:12,460
So since we already have
some [? input ?] examples,

1090
00:54:12,460 --> 00:54:15,780
we can filter the programs
that fail the given test

1091
00:54:15,780 --> 00:54:16,960
cases already.

1092
00:54:16,960 --> 00:54:18,750
But for the remaining
programs, they

1093
00:54:18,750 --> 00:54:21,200
might still fail on the
held-out test cases.

1094
00:54:21,200 --> 00:54:26,290
Like for those of you who have
participated in these coding

1095
00:54:26,290 --> 00:54:28,220
contests, you might
feel the same way.

1096
00:54:28,220 --> 00:54:29,630
You feel that your
code is great.

1097
00:54:29,630 --> 00:54:33,160
But it just fail on some
mysterious held-out test cases

1098
00:54:33,160 --> 00:54:35,950
and you didn't get
the score you want.

1099
00:54:35,950 --> 00:54:39,700
So basically, for the
AlphaCode system, what we do

1100
00:54:39,700 --> 00:54:43,150
is that we will train an LLM
model to generate new test

1101
00:54:43,150 --> 00:54:47,500
inputs for these test problems.

1102
00:54:47,500 --> 00:54:50,050
Then we will execute
those like sample programs

1103
00:54:50,050 --> 00:54:52,970
on all these model
generated inputs.

1104
00:54:52,970 --> 00:54:57,520
And then we cluster all programs
with the same outputs together.

1105
00:54:57,520 --> 00:55:00,010
The assumption here
is basically if we

1106
00:55:00,010 --> 00:55:04,460
have the model to generate
enough number of test inputs.

1107
00:55:04,460 --> 00:55:07,820
And also, they are
diverse of higher quality.

1108
00:55:07,820 --> 00:55:10,150
Then actually, we can
assume that all programs

1109
00:55:10,150 --> 00:55:12,070
in the same cluster
are semantically

1110
00:55:12,070 --> 00:55:14,820
equivalent because they lead
to the same execution results.

1111
00:55:17,470 --> 00:55:19,310
And then finally, we
sample one program

1112
00:55:19,310 --> 00:55:22,310
from each of the 10
largest clusters.

1113
00:55:22,310 --> 00:55:25,130
In our [? evaluation, ?] we show
that this clustering provides

1114
00:55:25,130 --> 00:55:26,900
additional performance
gain compared

1115
00:55:26,900 --> 00:55:28,590
to the filter only method.

1116
00:55:28,590 --> 00:55:31,950
But clearly, there is still a
gap from the Oracle selection.

1117
00:55:31,950 --> 00:55:33,650
Because there's
still no guarantee

1118
00:55:33,650 --> 00:55:36,440
that the consistent response
will be just the best

1119
00:55:36,440 --> 00:55:39,620
one out of the candidate pools.

1120
00:55:39,620 --> 00:55:42,650
Now let's revisit this
self-consistency decoding

1121
00:55:42,650 --> 00:55:43,870
pipeline.

1122
00:55:43,870 --> 00:55:45,890
So one limitation
here is that for

1123
00:55:45,890 --> 00:55:49,190
the original self-consistency,
we need an answer extraction

1124
00:55:49,190 --> 00:55:52,910
process so that we can do this
aggregation among the candidate

1125
00:55:52,910 --> 00:55:54,620
responses.

1126
00:55:54,620 --> 00:55:57,620
So if we want to apply
this consistency based

1127
00:55:57,620 --> 00:56:03,560
decoding to broader used cases,
the natural next question

1128
00:56:03,560 --> 00:56:06,470
is how we can do it for
free-form generation, where

1129
00:56:06,470 --> 00:56:09,950
it is not that natural to have
this aggregation and answer

1130
00:56:09,950 --> 00:56:12,080
extraction process?

1131
00:56:12,080 --> 00:56:14,390
So this is we want to
achieve with our work

1132
00:56:14,390 --> 00:56:16,680
on universal self-consistency.

1133
00:56:16,680 --> 00:56:19,990
The idea is that, instead of
having this answer extraction

1134
00:56:19,990 --> 00:56:23,170
process, we ask the large
language model to perform

1135
00:56:23,170 --> 00:56:25,100
consistency-based selection.

1136
00:56:25,100 --> 00:56:27,400
So specifically, we
throw an instruction

1137
00:56:27,400 --> 00:56:30,910
to the model asking it to select
the most consistent response

1138
00:56:30,910 --> 00:56:32,960
based on the majority consensus.

1139
00:56:32,960 --> 00:56:35,410
And also, it needs to take a
look at all these candidate

1140
00:56:35,410 --> 00:56:36,940
responses.

1141
00:56:36,940 --> 00:56:39,460
So the intuition here
is that, clearly, we

1142
00:56:39,460 --> 00:56:42,190
know that it will be hard for
the model to judge the answer

1143
00:56:42,190 --> 00:56:43,610
correctness by itself.

1144
00:56:43,610 --> 00:56:46,890
But consistency should be a
simpler criterion to measure.

1145
00:56:49,750 --> 00:56:51,430
Then empirically,
we also evaluate

1146
00:56:51,430 --> 00:56:53,380
this universal
self-consistency method

1147
00:56:53,380 --> 00:56:55,365
for different applications.

1148
00:56:55,365 --> 00:56:58,120
So for [? low-stack, ?]
free-form generation problems,

1149
00:56:58,120 --> 00:57:01,390
like summarization and
question answering,

1150
00:57:01,390 --> 00:57:06,050
we stack low [? factual ?]
like final answers.

1151
00:57:06,050 --> 00:57:09,280
We see that the universal
self-consistency also still

1152
00:57:09,280 --> 00:57:12,550
can improve over
the baselines, where

1153
00:57:12,550 --> 00:57:17,150
the original self-consistency
is not directly applicable.

1154
00:57:17,150 --> 00:57:19,430
An old math reasoning
and coding problems

1155
00:57:19,430 --> 00:57:22,950
where we can apply this
original self-consistency idea.

1156
00:57:22,950 --> 00:57:24,890
Actually, universal
self-consistency

1157
00:57:24,890 --> 00:57:27,890
can match its performance
without the requirement

1158
00:57:27,890 --> 00:57:33,140
of performing this answer
extraction and code execution.

1159
00:57:33,140 --> 00:57:36,500
And notice here, clearly,
this universal self-consistent

1160
00:57:36,500 --> 00:57:39,780
performance is bounded by
the long-context capability.

1161
00:57:39,780 --> 00:57:43,140
So at that time, we are
using an earlier model,

1162
00:57:43,140 --> 00:57:45,840
which is not super great
at long-context reasoning.

1163
00:57:45,840 --> 00:57:49,660
So we can see that when
we include more responses

1164
00:57:49,660 --> 00:57:52,010
in the prompt, the
performance might not

1165
00:57:52,010 --> 00:57:57,580
go up as well as the
original self-consistency.

1166
00:57:57,580 --> 00:58:00,020
But we can see that with
the recent development

1167
00:58:00,020 --> 00:58:01,810
of long-context models.

1168
00:58:01,810 --> 00:58:05,960
This will allow such kind of
model-based selection approach

1169
00:58:05,960 --> 00:58:08,100
to scale more with
more responses.

1170
00:58:08,100 --> 00:58:10,550
And also, in practice,
for most tasks,

1171
00:58:10,550 --> 00:58:12,960
it can be well-solved
within, let's say,

1172
00:58:12,960 --> 00:58:15,255
a 10 responses in
the candidate pool.

1173
00:58:15,255 --> 00:58:19,570
So this will still showcase the
usability of this model based

1174
00:58:19,570 --> 00:58:21,640
consistency selection.

1175
00:58:21,640 --> 00:58:25,900
Previously, we talked about,
for the model-based selection,

1176
00:58:25,900 --> 00:58:29,875
using the log probability
from the original model,

1177
00:58:29,875 --> 00:58:32,950
this approach of ranking
doesn't scale better

1178
00:58:32,950 --> 00:58:35,785
than the consistency-based
selection.

1179
00:58:35,785 --> 00:58:39,410
So to improve further over this
consistency-based selection,

1180
00:58:39,410 --> 00:58:42,280
one idea is that we can
train a large language

1181
00:58:42,280 --> 00:58:44,060
model as the ranker.

1182
00:58:44,060 --> 00:58:47,590
And we hope that at
least this ranker

1183
00:58:47,590 --> 00:58:51,350
can perform better than this
simple consistency criterion.

1184
00:58:51,350 --> 00:58:55,840
And actually, it can have some
kind of sense of which responses

1185
00:58:55,840 --> 00:58:58,360
are more likely to be accurate.

1186
00:58:58,360 --> 00:59:02,440
So here, on the slides,
I'm showing this diagram

1187
00:59:02,440 --> 00:59:05,200
from the original
OpenAI paper, which

1188
00:59:05,200 --> 00:59:08,860
introduced the GSM8K data set.

1189
00:59:08,860 --> 00:59:11,050
And also, they
demonstrate this method

1190
00:59:11,050 --> 00:59:15,230
of how to train a verifier to
show the-- to basically judge

1191
00:59:15,230 --> 00:59:17,870
the correctness of
the matched solution.

1192
00:59:17,870 --> 00:59:20,990
And also, later on, they have
this follow up paper called

1193
00:59:20,990 --> 00:59:22,470
"Let's Verify Step by Step."

1194
00:59:22,470 --> 00:59:27,090
So in total, for this line
of LLM-based ranking works,

1195
00:59:27,090 --> 00:59:32,600
there are two ways to
design the LLM-based method

1196
00:59:32,600 --> 00:59:35,870
to judge the answer quality.

1197
00:59:35,870 --> 00:59:38,000
The first way is the most--

1198
00:59:38,000 --> 00:59:40,310
the initial and the most
standard approach, which is

1199
00:59:40,310 --> 00:59:42,780
called outcome-supervised
reward model.

1200
00:59:42,780 --> 00:59:44,840
Basically, it will
verify the correctness

1201
00:59:44,840 --> 00:59:46,110
at the solution level.

1202
00:59:46,110 --> 00:59:49,100
Like given a math problem,
the solution, the model

1203
00:59:49,100 --> 00:59:51,970
will tell you whether the
solution is correct or not.

1204
00:59:54,800 --> 00:59:58,910
Another approach introduced in
this "Let's Verify Step by Step"

1205
00:59:58,910 --> 01:00:02,520
paper is called
process-supervised reward model.

1206
01:00:02,520 --> 01:00:05,720
So the idea is that, instead of
verifying [? LLMs ?] solution

1207
01:00:05,720 --> 01:00:08,750
space, we can also
very, at a step level,

1208
01:00:08,750 --> 01:00:13,345
we can see whether
each step is accurate.

1209
01:00:13,345 --> 01:00:18,190
So in their work, they show that
this process-supervised reward

1210
01:00:18,190 --> 01:00:21,220
model actually scales
better with more samples

1211
01:00:21,220 --> 01:00:24,490
compared to this outcome-based
reward model and also

1212
01:00:24,490 --> 01:00:27,730
this majority voting baseline.

1213
01:00:27,730 --> 01:00:29,500
But clearly, here,
the performance

1214
01:00:29,500 --> 01:00:32,710
will be highly dependent
on the verifier quality.

1215
01:00:32,710 --> 01:00:35,110
And also, the same
verifier might not

1216
01:00:35,110 --> 01:00:36,470
generalize across tasks.

1217
01:00:36,470 --> 01:00:38,920
Because we know that this
consistency-based criterion is

1218
01:00:38,920 --> 01:00:42,200
very simple and actually can be
directly applied to many tasks.

1219
01:00:42,200 --> 01:00:44,170
But for these
[? rank ?] models, you

1220
01:00:44,170 --> 01:00:46,790
need to design a good
training strategy

1221
01:00:46,790 --> 01:00:50,155
and also design a good training
data so that it can really

1222
01:00:50,155 --> 01:00:53,140
work across different
domains you care about.

1223
01:00:53,140 --> 01:00:55,660
So far, we have talked
about response selection

1224
01:00:55,660 --> 01:00:58,010
only at the solution level.

1225
01:00:58,010 --> 01:00:59,590
So we only perform
this selection

1226
01:00:59,590 --> 01:01:03,250
after the full
responses are generated.

1227
01:01:03,250 --> 01:01:07,125
But assuming that we have a
very good step-wise scorer,

1228
01:01:07,125 --> 01:01:09,620
then actually, this
solution-based response

1229
01:01:09,620 --> 01:01:13,760
selection is a waste of
using this step-wise scorer.

1230
01:01:13,760 --> 01:01:17,450
Because actually, we can
do more with such kind

1231
01:01:17,450 --> 01:01:21,875
of a more powerful and
more fine-grained verifier.

1232
01:01:21,875 --> 01:01:25,650
So if we really have this good
step-wise scorer function,

1233
01:01:25,650 --> 01:01:28,100
then actually, we can
perform this tree search

1234
01:01:28,100 --> 01:01:29,520
with large language model.

1235
01:01:29,520 --> 01:01:33,200
Where basically, instead of
waiting until the solution

1236
01:01:33,200 --> 01:01:36,770
becomes complete, during
the search process,

1237
01:01:36,770 --> 01:01:40,400
we can prioritize those
exploration of partial solutions

1238
01:01:40,400 --> 01:01:41,550
at the step level.

1239
01:01:41,550 --> 01:01:44,705
And we can explore
more promising steps

1240
01:01:44,705 --> 01:01:48,800
before exploring
other [? processes. ?]

1241
01:01:48,800 --> 01:01:52,220
So on the slides, I'm
showing this list,

1242
01:01:52,220 --> 01:01:55,800
like diagram from the Tree
of Thought prompting paper.

1243
01:01:55,800 --> 01:01:58,550
So from this illustration,
you can clearly

1244
01:01:58,550 --> 01:02:02,000
see what are the differences
of different prompting methods.

1245
01:02:02,000 --> 01:02:05,750
Like Chain-of-Thought
prompting is basically a train.

1246
01:02:05,750 --> 01:02:08,950
This self-consistency method is
like multiple parallel chains.

1247
01:02:08,950 --> 01:02:10,540
And then for this
Train of Thought,

1248
01:02:10,540 --> 01:02:13,980
basically, at each step, it
can have multiple branches

1249
01:02:13,980 --> 01:02:15,760
to explore the next step.

1250
01:02:15,760 --> 01:02:18,120
And you don't have to
complete all these step

1251
01:02:18,120 --> 01:02:20,640
passes until the end.

1252
01:02:20,640 --> 01:02:23,280
So here, I will
show this how we can

1253
01:02:23,280 --> 01:02:26,730
apply this Tree of Thought idea
with a simple example of game

1254
01:02:26,730 --> 01:02:27,760
of 24.

1255
01:02:27,760 --> 01:02:29,670
So basically, at
each step, there

1256
01:02:29,670 --> 01:02:33,250
are two important
stages we want to do.

1257
01:02:33,250 --> 01:02:35,470
One is called
thought generation.

1258
01:02:35,470 --> 01:02:39,330
Basically, in this step, we will
prompt the large language model

1259
01:02:39,330 --> 01:02:42,060
to propose possible
next thinking steps.

1260
01:02:42,060 --> 01:02:44,820
Like in the game of 24,
these next thinking steps

1261
01:02:44,820 --> 01:02:46,930
are pretty simple in format.

1262
01:02:46,930 --> 01:02:48,720
You just need to
select the two numbers

1263
01:02:48,720 --> 01:02:51,630
to perform this calculation.

1264
01:02:51,630 --> 01:02:54,540
Then there is another stage
called thought evaluation.

1265
01:02:54,540 --> 01:02:56,880
Where again, here, we can
perform the large language

1266
01:02:56,880 --> 01:03:00,030
model to evaluate how
promising the current state is.

1267
01:03:00,030 --> 01:03:02,190
Like for the game
of 24, you need

1268
01:03:02,190 --> 01:03:06,835
to decide whether it is possible
to reach the final number of 24

1269
01:03:06,835 --> 01:03:10,040
after the list, all
the manipulations.

1270
01:03:10,040 --> 01:03:12,950
So previously, we show how--

1271
01:03:12,950 --> 01:03:15,140
we can show how we
can do this [? text ?]

1272
01:03:15,140 --> 01:03:18,450
evaluation at step level.

1273
01:03:18,450 --> 01:03:22,940
So it will determine the
quality of each individual step.

1274
01:03:22,940 --> 01:03:26,720
But related to our previous
consistency-based selection

1275
01:03:26,720 --> 01:03:29,790
discussion for this
scoring function,

1276
01:03:29,790 --> 01:03:31,730
we can also think
about it as a way

1277
01:03:31,730 --> 01:03:34,590
to just select the best
state among the candidates.

1278
01:03:34,590 --> 01:03:36,260
We don't need to
just have the model

1279
01:03:36,260 --> 01:03:39,620
to make this decision
at the point level.

1280
01:03:39,620 --> 01:03:42,380
So the idea is that we
can ask the large language

1281
01:03:42,380 --> 01:03:46,700
model to select the
best responses among all

1282
01:03:46,700 --> 01:03:49,610
these possible next steps.

1283
01:03:49,610 --> 01:03:52,770
And then, we can also do
this voting multiple times.

1284
01:03:52,770 --> 01:03:55,325
Because since all
these [INAUDIBLE]

1285
01:03:55,325 --> 01:03:57,990
here is based on large
language model sampling.

1286
01:03:57,990 --> 01:04:00,060
Then with all these
different votes,

1287
01:04:00,060 --> 01:04:03,150
the model can finally
select the majority vote

1288
01:04:03,150 --> 01:04:04,925
as the final choice to proceed.

1289
01:04:08,495 --> 01:04:11,460
Here, for the results,
I'm showing the list again

1290
01:04:11,460 --> 01:04:12,850
of 24 evaluation.

1291
01:04:12,850 --> 01:04:16,440
And in the original paper, they
also show some other benchmarks.

1292
01:04:16,440 --> 01:04:19,080
So we can see that
with Tree of Thought

1293
01:04:19,080 --> 01:04:21,450
using breadth-first
search, it scales

1294
01:04:21,450 --> 01:04:24,000
better than the standard
prompting and Chain-of-Thought

1295
01:04:24,000 --> 01:04:25,880
with respect to token budget.

1296
01:04:29,130 --> 01:04:32,250
And going further, clearly, we
can integrate some more advanced

1297
01:04:32,250 --> 01:04:33,390
search algorithms.

1298
01:04:33,390 --> 01:04:36,060
Like in this original
Tree of Thought paper,

1299
01:04:36,060 --> 01:04:40,150
they use the most basic BFS
and [? DFS ?] algorithms.

1300
01:04:40,150 --> 01:04:43,440
But in the later one,
we also see some papers

1301
01:04:43,440 --> 01:04:46,780
which are coming up with these
Monte-Carlo Tree Search methods.

1302
01:04:46,780 --> 01:04:49,800
And some works are also trying
to train a separate evaluation

1303
01:04:49,800 --> 01:04:53,540
function instead of just
prompting the model.

1304
01:04:53,540 --> 01:04:55,470
Again here, the
important consideration

1305
01:04:55,470 --> 01:04:58,710
is that we need to design
a good language model

1306
01:04:58,710 --> 01:05:00,980
and prompting scheme
so that it can be

1307
01:05:00,980 --> 01:05:05,405
effective for self-evaluation.

1308
01:05:05,405 --> 01:05:08,772
So to summarize, in
this second part,

1309
01:05:08,772 --> 01:05:11,450
I show that we can further
scale this inference time

1310
01:05:11,450 --> 01:05:16,760
compute by sampling multiple
branches in the solution space.

1311
01:05:16,760 --> 01:05:19,260
And we talked about
consistency-based selection,

1312
01:05:19,260 --> 01:05:24,110
which is a pretty simple, but
effective and general principle.

1313
01:05:24,110 --> 01:05:26,150
We talked about this
self-consistency,

1314
01:05:26,150 --> 01:05:28,670
where we can marginalize
out reasoning processes

1315
01:05:28,670 --> 01:05:31,850
and do the selection-based
on the final answer.

1316
01:05:31,850 --> 01:05:34,760
And for code generation, we
can also do this reranking

1317
01:05:34,760 --> 01:05:39,350
based on execution consistency.

1318
01:05:39,350 --> 01:05:41,510
When this large language
model self-evaluation

1319
01:05:41,510 --> 01:05:43,640
works well so that
it can really judge

1320
01:05:43,640 --> 01:05:47,630
the quality of partial
solutions or structure quality

1321
01:05:47,630 --> 01:05:48,870
at a step level.

1322
01:05:48,870 --> 01:05:51,210
We can also apply
this search method.

1323
01:05:51,210 --> 01:05:54,540
And in this case, sometimes,
it will scale better.

1324
01:05:54,540 --> 01:05:57,200
And also, it can
reduce the token cost

1325
01:05:57,200 --> 01:06:00,690
because it can already
eliminates those nonpromising

1326
01:06:00,690 --> 01:06:02,940
[? process ?] during
the mean time.

1327
01:06:02,940 --> 01:06:06,250
So finally, I will talk about
iterative self-improvement,

1328
01:06:06,250 --> 01:06:08,130
which will try to
increase the depth

1329
01:06:08,130 --> 01:06:09,395
to reach the final solution.

1330
01:06:12,540 --> 01:06:16,060
So even with the most
recent advanced models,

1331
01:06:16,060 --> 01:06:18,000
including those
recent models, we

1332
01:06:18,000 --> 01:06:21,780
can see that they can achieve
pretty amazing numbers

1333
01:06:21,780 --> 01:06:26,070
on those competitive and
challenging benchmarks

1334
01:06:26,070 --> 01:06:28,360
for solving hard maths
and coding questions.

1335
01:06:28,360 --> 01:06:31,290
But they can still, sometimes,
make pretty obvious mistakes

1336
01:06:31,290 --> 01:06:34,050
and people are keeping
complaining about it.

1337
01:06:34,050 --> 01:06:36,370
But if you think
about us as humans,

1338
01:06:36,370 --> 01:06:39,510
we also tend to make sometimes
pretty trivial mistakes

1339
01:06:39,510 --> 01:06:40,400
at first thought.

1340
01:06:43,350 --> 01:06:46,920
So previously, we talked about
we can sample multiple solutions

1341
01:06:46,920 --> 01:06:51,030
to help us reduce these mistakes
from a single prediction.

1342
01:06:51,030 --> 01:06:52,950
But if we think
about it again, it

1343
01:06:52,950 --> 01:06:57,475
is actually a pretty suboptimal
way to do this error correction.

1344
01:06:57,475 --> 01:07:00,420
Because all these responses
are generated in parallel.

1345
01:07:00,420 --> 01:07:02,840
So we do not really learn
from the past mistakes,

1346
01:07:02,840 --> 01:07:05,300
we can just have
the model generate

1347
01:07:05,300 --> 01:07:08,350
the same wrong predictions
again and again.

1348
01:07:11,570 --> 01:07:15,540
So basically, in this part about
inference time self-improvement,

1349
01:07:15,540 --> 01:07:19,070
I will talk about techniques
using large language models

1350
01:07:19,070 --> 01:07:23,870
to iteratively improve its own
response for the given task.

1351
01:07:23,870 --> 01:07:26,540
And also, this will
align better with us

1352
01:07:26,540 --> 01:07:28,510
humans like an error
correction process.

1353
01:07:31,610 --> 01:07:33,860
So the first papers
about these ideas

1354
01:07:33,860 --> 01:07:37,475
include this like
reflection and self-refine.

1355
01:07:37,475 --> 01:07:39,650
So basically, in
this process, there

1356
01:07:39,650 --> 01:07:44,660
are also two important steps
after generating each solution.

1357
01:07:44,660 --> 01:07:47,130
So basically, after the
model generates the solution,

1358
01:07:47,130 --> 01:07:49,550
the model will also
generate some feedback

1359
01:07:49,550 --> 01:07:51,800
based on what it observes.

1360
01:07:51,800 --> 01:07:54,350
And in this step, it can use
those external evaluation

1361
01:07:54,350 --> 01:07:55,660
when available.

1362
01:07:55,660 --> 01:07:58,360
So for example, in the
original reflection paper,

1363
01:07:58,360 --> 01:08:02,070
they focus more on this
agentic setup, where basically,

1364
01:08:02,070 --> 01:08:04,740
the LLM, as the agent
will propose actions

1365
01:08:04,740 --> 01:08:05,770
to the environment.

1366
01:08:05,770 --> 01:08:10,140
And the environment will
give the model some kind

1367
01:08:10,140 --> 01:08:12,610
of changes of the observations.

1368
01:08:12,610 --> 01:08:15,030
So this will be
external signal to tell

1369
01:08:15,030 --> 01:08:18,300
the model whether the current
[? state ?] is promising.

1370
01:08:18,300 --> 01:08:22,260
Then based on this model
generated reflection feedback

1371
01:08:22,260 --> 01:08:24,824
and also these
external observations,

1372
01:08:24,824 --> 01:08:27,870
then this large language
model will refine its output

1373
01:08:27,870 --> 01:08:31,590
to revise this next
prediction step given

1374
01:08:31,590 --> 01:08:36,450
both this internal
and external signals.

1375
01:08:36,450 --> 01:08:38,609
In this reflection
paper, they show

1376
01:08:38,609 --> 01:08:41,040
that this self-reflection
and self-refinement

1377
01:08:41,040 --> 01:08:45,059
works pretty well, especially
when we can have a scheme.

1378
01:08:45,059 --> 01:08:48,990
So that basically, the model
can utilize pretty high quality

1379
01:08:48,990 --> 01:08:54,835
evaluation or when the external
signals are very reliable.

1380
01:08:54,835 --> 01:08:57,800
So for example, in the
case of a ALFWorld,

1381
01:08:57,800 --> 01:09:01,430
it is an agent, like
a navigation task.

1382
01:09:01,430 --> 01:09:06,069
So there will be clear signals
about observations at each step.

1383
01:09:06,069 --> 01:09:10,569
And they also evaluate on
their approach on HotPotQA.

1384
01:09:10,569 --> 01:09:13,689
But they kind of
repurpose this QA task

1385
01:09:13,689 --> 01:09:15,290
into an agent environment.

1386
01:09:15,290 --> 01:09:18,680
In the sense that after the
model generates the solution,

1387
01:09:18,680 --> 01:09:20,080
they assume that
this environment

1388
01:09:20,080 --> 01:09:23,050
will give a signal about whether
the current solution is correct

1389
01:09:23,050 --> 01:09:24,160
or not.

1390
01:09:24,160 --> 01:09:27,790
This is why you can keep seeing
this improvement with more

1391
01:09:27,790 --> 01:09:28,810
trials.

1392
01:09:28,810 --> 01:09:30,765
And we will revisit
this assumption later.

1393
01:09:34,359 --> 01:09:36,760
And if we think about this
like self-improvement loop

1394
01:09:36,760 --> 01:09:39,520
for applications,
then code generation

1395
01:09:39,520 --> 01:09:42,339
is actually a pretty
natural used case.

1396
01:09:42,339 --> 01:09:46,670
Because when we are writing
code, for us for example,

1397
01:09:46,670 --> 01:09:49,580
we also debug our code
better within an IDE.

1398
01:09:49,580 --> 01:09:52,100
And we do not just write
the code and we stop.

1399
01:09:52,100 --> 01:09:55,200
We actually really rely
on this interactive loop

1400
01:09:55,200 --> 01:09:58,830
when we are trying to
investigate all these code

1401
01:09:58,830 --> 01:09:59,910
execution, results.

1402
01:09:59,910 --> 01:10:02,580
And see whether it makes
sense and also which

1403
01:10:02,580 --> 01:10:04,540
parts we should revise later.

1404
01:10:04,540 --> 01:10:07,620
And this is also
one fundamental idea

1405
01:10:07,620 --> 01:10:10,845
of the recent coding agents.

1406
01:10:10,845 --> 01:10:12,790
So in our paper
of self-debugging,

1407
01:10:12,790 --> 01:10:17,100
we also do a deeper dive into
what kind of feedback formats

1408
01:10:17,100 --> 01:10:21,945
will be helpful for the model to
better correct its own mistakes.

1409
01:10:21,945 --> 01:10:27,040
So for example, the most
simple feedback message

1410
01:10:27,040 --> 01:10:30,120
is basically a short
universal information

1411
01:10:30,120 --> 01:10:32,400
about whether a current
code is correct or not

1412
01:10:32,400 --> 01:10:35,130
without more information.

1413
01:10:35,130 --> 01:10:37,440
And then for unit
test feedback, we also

1414
01:10:37,440 --> 01:10:39,400
include the execution results.

1415
01:10:39,400 --> 01:10:42,330
Like whether the current
unit test has passed

1416
01:10:42,330 --> 01:10:46,560
or whether there are
any runtime errors.

1417
01:10:46,560 --> 01:10:49,170
Then for code explanation,
basically, at each step,

1418
01:10:49,170 --> 01:10:52,240
we also ask the model to
self-generate some line

1419
01:10:52,240 --> 01:10:54,850
by line explanation
of the implementation

1420
01:10:54,850 --> 01:10:56,030
in natural language.

1421
01:10:56,030 --> 01:10:59,080
So this idea is that kind
of a motivated by human

1422
01:10:59,080 --> 01:11:01,300
[INAUDIBLE] debugging,
trying to ask the model

1423
01:11:01,300 --> 01:11:06,550
to see what it's trying
to do in the code.

1424
01:11:06,550 --> 01:11:08,270
And finally, in
the trace feedback.

1425
01:11:08,270 --> 01:11:11,620
Basically, we ask the
model to perform some line

1426
01:11:11,620 --> 01:11:14,980
by line simulation of the
execution trace by itself.

1427
01:11:14,980 --> 01:11:18,350
This is also one way of
debugging by human programmers.

1428
01:11:20,890 --> 01:11:26,290
Empirically, we evaluated the
latest large language models

1429
01:11:26,290 --> 01:11:29,300
at the time of paper
publication last year.

1430
01:11:29,300 --> 01:11:32,240
So basically, we can see that
with this self-debugging loop,

1431
01:11:32,240 --> 01:11:34,090
it consistently
improves the performance

1432
01:11:34,090 --> 01:11:35,710
across different
large language models

1433
01:11:35,710 --> 01:11:37,540
of different capabilities.

1434
01:11:37,540 --> 01:11:40,430
And also, if we give more
informative feedback,

1435
01:11:40,430 --> 01:11:42,890
it can further improve
the debugging performance.

1436
01:11:46,165 --> 01:11:49,710
Now let's go back to this
self-correction for question

1437
01:11:49,710 --> 01:11:51,330
answering tasks.

1438
01:11:51,330 --> 01:11:54,690
Previously, we talked about
these results of HotPotQA.

1439
01:11:54,690 --> 01:11:57,540
And also, in some
other works, it also

1440
01:11:57,540 --> 01:12:00,480
show this improvement
of self-correction

1441
01:12:00,480 --> 01:12:04,440
on other reasoning task
like math problem solving.

1442
01:12:04,440 --> 01:12:07,530
But all of these
significant improvement

1443
01:12:07,530 --> 01:12:10,835
comes from the assumption
with an Oracle verifier.

1444
01:12:13,650 --> 01:12:15,840
But in practice,
this Oracle verifier

1445
01:12:15,840 --> 01:12:19,050
will not be available
in many scenarios.

1446
01:12:19,050 --> 01:12:21,970
Especially, in the case of
solving, reasoning problems.

1447
01:12:21,970 --> 01:12:24,700
You can imagine that when
you are taking a math test,

1448
01:12:24,700 --> 01:12:27,130
you don't really have the
official solution at hand.

1449
01:12:27,130 --> 01:12:28,710
Otherwise, it will
be just trivial

1450
01:12:28,710 --> 01:12:30,670
for you to finish the exam.

1451
01:12:30,670 --> 01:12:33,570
So basically, the question
here we want to study

1452
01:12:33,570 --> 01:12:35,730
is how do these
large language models

1453
01:12:35,730 --> 01:12:39,540
perform if we do not
give the model such very

1454
01:12:39,540 --> 01:12:43,110
explicit and accurate external
feedback about the response

1455
01:12:43,110 --> 01:12:46,380
correctness.

1456
01:12:46,380 --> 01:12:50,440
So in our paper published
last year at ICLR called Large

1457
01:12:50,440 --> 01:12:53,410
Language Models Cannot
Self-Correct Reasoning Yet,

1458
01:12:53,410 --> 01:12:58,060
we actually show some negative
results from this study.

1459
01:12:58,060 --> 01:13:00,670
So we compare with
this Oracle baseline,

1460
01:13:00,670 --> 01:13:04,340
where we utilize the ground
truth answer for question.

1461
01:13:04,340 --> 01:13:10,300
And same as previous works,
we show this huge improvement.

1462
01:13:10,300 --> 01:13:14,180
Then we studied the case of
the real self-correction,

1463
01:13:14,180 --> 01:13:17,990
where we do not allow the model
to utilize any Oracle feedback.

1464
01:13:17,990 --> 01:13:20,290
The model needs to judge
the response correctness

1465
01:13:20,290 --> 01:13:22,060
by themselves.

1466
01:13:22,060 --> 01:13:25,390
Then these are the results,
we show in the bottom table.

1467
01:13:25,390 --> 01:13:27,310
We can see that
the main issue here

1468
01:13:27,310 --> 01:13:29,860
is that because large
language models can only

1469
01:13:29,860 --> 01:13:32,090
judge the correctness
of its predictions,

1470
01:13:32,090 --> 01:13:35,080
it often turns an
originally correct solution

1471
01:13:35,080 --> 01:13:36,420
into a wrong one.

1472
01:13:36,420 --> 01:13:39,770
So this is why actually
in this self-correction

1473
01:13:39,770 --> 01:13:42,910
without external
evaluation, this process

1474
01:13:42,910 --> 01:13:45,590
can lead to worse
performance after each round.

1475
01:13:49,230 --> 01:13:51,930
So with this study,
one natural question

1476
01:13:51,930 --> 01:13:55,260
is probably we didn't tune
the feedback prompt well

1477
01:13:55,260 --> 01:13:57,095
so this is why it fails.

1478
01:13:57,095 --> 01:14:01,335
So to address this
question, we also

1479
01:14:01,335 --> 01:14:05,780
design several variants
of this feedback prompt.

1480
01:14:05,780 --> 01:14:07,530
And notice that all
these feedback prompts

1481
01:14:07,530 --> 01:14:08,850
are pretty general.

1482
01:14:08,850 --> 01:14:12,190
They are not specific
tuned for one task.

1483
01:14:12,190 --> 01:14:15,690
So we can see that if we just
have this simple general-purpose

1484
01:14:15,690 --> 01:14:18,780
feedback prompt, if we do not
have any more of the model

1485
01:14:18,780 --> 01:14:22,500
training, then editing this
feedback prompt, indeed,

1486
01:14:22,500 --> 01:14:24,700
can affect the
self-correction behavior.

1487
01:14:24,700 --> 01:14:28,710
But this is more about adjusting
the tendency for the model

1488
01:14:28,710 --> 01:14:30,980
to keep the initial response.

1489
01:14:30,980 --> 01:14:34,920
So in some cases
actually, this feedback,

1490
01:14:34,920 --> 01:14:36,760
after the feedback
prompt update,

1491
01:14:36,760 --> 01:14:39,670
there's no much regression
on the reasoning performance.

1492
01:14:39,670 --> 01:14:42,240
But then in this case,
this also doesn't

1493
01:14:42,240 --> 01:14:45,550
help the model to improve
over the initial performance.

1494
01:14:45,550 --> 01:14:48,650
So previously, we discussed
about this comparison

1495
01:14:48,650 --> 01:14:50,170
if we just do this
self-correction

1496
01:14:50,170 --> 01:14:52,150
over one initial response.

1497
01:14:52,150 --> 01:14:54,880
And we further compare
with another method

1498
01:14:54,880 --> 01:14:57,040
where basically we do
this self-correction

1499
01:14:57,040 --> 01:14:59,185
among multiple responses.

1500
01:14:59,185 --> 01:15:02,870
So for this, we compare with
the multi-agent debate baseline.

1501
01:15:02,870 --> 01:15:06,390
The idea is that we will
have the model generate

1502
01:15:06,390 --> 01:15:08,540
several responses
in parallel once.

1503
01:15:08,540 --> 01:15:10,300
Then we perform the
large language model

1504
01:15:10,300 --> 01:15:12,430
to reveal these
multiple responses

1505
01:15:12,430 --> 01:15:15,430
and give an updated one.

1506
01:15:15,430 --> 01:15:17,170
And for this
multi-agent debate, we

1507
01:15:17,170 --> 01:15:19,370
will compare with
self-consistency.

1508
01:15:19,370 --> 01:15:21,940
Recall that self-consistency
is just completely generated

1509
01:15:21,940 --> 01:15:24,490
solutions in parallel
and select the response

1510
01:15:24,490 --> 01:15:28,615
with the most
common final answer.

1511
01:15:28,615 --> 01:15:31,220
So in the previous
multi-agent debate papers,

1512
01:15:31,220 --> 01:15:32,800
they are showing
some improvement

1513
01:15:32,800 --> 01:15:34,400
over self-consistency.

1514
01:15:34,400 --> 01:15:36,220
But we find that
the major issue is

1515
01:15:36,220 --> 01:15:40,450
that they do not have a very
rigorous comparison in terms

1516
01:15:40,450 --> 01:15:42,080
of the token budget.

1517
01:15:42,080 --> 01:15:45,450
So actually, because in
the multi-agent debate,

1518
01:15:45,450 --> 01:15:50,165
each round for this specific
experiment has three responses.

1519
01:15:50,165 --> 01:15:53,600
But basically, if we keep
the same number of response

1520
01:15:53,600 --> 01:15:57,380
and budget, we will
see that actually still

1521
01:15:57,380 --> 01:16:01,320
self-consistency scales better
than the multi-agent debate.

1522
01:16:01,320 --> 01:16:04,190
Because at some point, again,
this multi-agent debate

1523
01:16:04,190 --> 01:16:06,420
performance stops improving.

1524
01:16:06,420 --> 01:16:09,080
But with more responses,
self-consistency performance

1525
01:16:09,080 --> 01:16:13,800
is still improving on this GSM8K
[? MATH ?] problem benchmark.

1526
01:16:17,090 --> 01:16:19,940
So given all this
discussion, then the question

1527
01:16:19,940 --> 01:16:23,030
here is how we can best
utilize the token budget

1528
01:16:23,030 --> 01:16:26,870
by integrating all these
different methods together.

1529
01:16:26,870 --> 01:16:29,570
So the fundamental question
is here, basically,

1530
01:16:29,570 --> 01:16:33,050
we need to understand how to
balance the inference budget

1531
01:16:33,050 --> 01:16:39,200
for generating multiple samples
in parallel or sequentially.

1532
01:16:39,200 --> 01:16:41,770
So actually, this is a
pretty difficult question

1533
01:16:41,770 --> 01:16:45,530
because it really depends
on the task and the model.

1534
01:16:45,530 --> 01:16:51,400
And in essence, it's about
whether the model for evaluation

1535
01:16:51,400 --> 01:16:54,190
is good at self-reflection
and correction

1536
01:16:54,190 --> 01:16:56,770
for your specific task.

1537
01:16:56,770 --> 01:16:59,950
So here, I'm showing an example
study from my colleagues

1538
01:16:59,950 --> 01:17:02,080
at Google, where
they are trying to--

1539
01:17:02,080 --> 01:17:04,340
basically, in this
specific experiments,

1540
01:17:04,340 --> 01:17:06,400
they are trying to
find the optimal way

1541
01:17:06,400 --> 01:17:09,710
to scale this token budget
to solve the math problems.

1542
01:17:09,710 --> 01:17:11,960
So actually here,
they trained a model,

1543
01:17:11,960 --> 01:17:15,730
which is supposed to be more
specialized at correcting

1544
01:17:15,730 --> 01:17:17,330
math solutions.

1545
01:17:17,330 --> 01:17:22,720
And also here, for the different
difficulty level of problems,

1546
01:17:22,720 --> 01:17:25,210
they study what's
the optimal ratio

1547
01:17:25,210 --> 01:17:28,270
to perform this
parallel generation

1548
01:17:28,270 --> 01:17:30,450
versus sequential generation.

1549
01:17:30,450 --> 01:17:33,790
So I would say this concrete
numbers will really vary

1550
01:17:33,790 --> 01:17:36,110
a lot among different models.

1551
01:17:36,110 --> 01:17:38,270
But basically here,
the overall conclusion

1552
01:17:38,270 --> 01:17:42,530
is that, for simple problems--

1553
01:17:42,530 --> 01:17:45,050
actually, the model can benefit
more from self-correction

1554
01:17:45,050 --> 01:17:47,990
because it knows
a more clear about

1555
01:17:47,990 --> 01:17:50,850
whether the current solution is
wrong and how it should revise.

1556
01:17:50,850 --> 01:17:52,940
But for harder problems,
actually, there

1557
01:17:52,940 --> 01:17:57,800
is a better point in the
middle about how much

1558
01:17:57,800 --> 01:17:59,480
you should perform
parallel generation.

1559
01:17:59,480 --> 01:18:03,380
And also, this
sequential refinement.

1560
01:18:03,380 --> 01:18:07,865
And then with this kind
of compute optimal factor

1561
01:18:07,865 --> 01:18:10,740
for different task
difficulty of problems,

1562
01:18:10,740 --> 01:18:14,000
they show that this compute
optimal curve can also

1563
01:18:14,000 --> 01:18:19,820
scale better than this
complete parallel generation.

1564
01:18:19,820 --> 01:18:22,290
And when we think about
inference time compute,

1565
01:18:22,290 --> 01:18:23,960
another factor we
should consider

1566
01:18:23,960 --> 01:18:26,005
is about the model size.

1567
01:18:26,005 --> 01:18:28,790
Because intuitively, with
the same FLOPs budget,

1568
01:18:28,790 --> 01:18:32,190
we can sample much more
solutions from a lighter model.

1569
01:18:32,190 --> 01:18:36,790
So more expensive model
does not perform much better

1570
01:18:36,790 --> 01:18:40,930
than a cheaper model, then it
will not be a very optimal way

1571
01:18:40,930 --> 01:18:43,665
to do the inference by calling
the more expensive model.

1572
01:18:46,315 --> 01:18:50,170
So basically, from
this empirical study

1573
01:18:50,170 --> 01:18:53,410
from the paper
inference scaling laws,

1574
01:18:53,410 --> 01:18:55,840
again, they will show that
with the optimal model

1575
01:18:55,840 --> 01:18:59,920
with different inference
budget can be a different.

1576
01:18:59,920 --> 01:19:02,840
Like when you have a smaller
inference time budget,

1577
01:19:02,840 --> 01:19:06,232
maybe you want to rely
more on the lighter models.

1578
01:19:06,232 --> 01:19:07,940
But basically, when
you have more budget,

1579
01:19:07,940 --> 01:19:10,730
probably you can utilize
the more expensive models,

1580
01:19:10,730 --> 01:19:13,210
which can probably give you
some boost on those very

1581
01:19:13,210 --> 01:19:14,110
challenging problems.

1582
01:19:14,110 --> 01:19:17,200
Where with the
smaller models, it

1583
01:19:17,200 --> 01:19:19,950
will be very hard to come up
with the final solution anyway.

1584
01:19:23,560 --> 01:19:25,120
So basically, in
this lecture, we

1585
01:19:25,120 --> 01:19:28,810
have covered different ways
to perform this inference time

1586
01:19:28,810 --> 01:19:31,610
scaling with the
more token budget.

1587
01:19:31,610 --> 01:19:34,520
So we talk about the
basic prompting techniques

1588
01:19:34,520 --> 01:19:37,700
about this search and
selection to increase

1589
01:19:37,700 --> 01:19:40,320
the width of exploring
the solution space.

1590
01:19:40,320 --> 01:19:45,050
And also, we talk about
iterative self-improvement.

1591
01:19:45,050 --> 01:19:48,830
And again, the best practice to
integrate with a large language

1592
01:19:48,830 --> 01:19:52,320
model should be adapted
according to its capabilities.

1593
01:19:52,320 --> 01:19:55,460
So you really need to consider
the underlying model capability

1594
01:19:55,460 --> 01:19:59,510
and also the task in your hand
to think about how you can best

1595
01:19:59,510 --> 01:20:01,340
combine all these
different techniques

1596
01:20:01,340 --> 01:20:06,770
and design a more efficient
and effective paradigm

1597
01:20:06,770 --> 01:20:09,770
for your task.

1598
01:20:09,770 --> 01:20:12,920
And finally, I also want to
talk about the general principle

1599
01:20:12,920 --> 01:20:16,140
of how to design effective
reasoning techniques.

1600
01:20:16,140 --> 01:20:18,980
And this actually will be
helpful for both inference time

1601
01:20:18,980 --> 01:20:21,650
techniques and training
time techniques.

1602
01:20:21,650 --> 01:20:25,100
So I guess many of you might
have heard about this The Bitter

1603
01:20:25,100 --> 01:20:26,850
Lesson from Richard Sutton.

1604
01:20:26,850 --> 01:20:31,280
But if not, I highly encourage
you to read this web page.

1605
01:20:31,280 --> 01:20:34,600
Actually, I will say this is
a very important guideline

1606
01:20:34,600 --> 01:20:38,480
for many of these modern
machine learning works.

1607
01:20:38,480 --> 01:20:41,050
And especially, with
the recent development

1608
01:20:41,050 --> 01:20:44,110
of large language models
and reasoning techniques.

1609
01:20:44,110 --> 01:20:47,450
So this article itself
has a lot of text.

1610
01:20:47,450 --> 01:20:51,730
But I will quote some most
interesting text in my opinion

1611
01:20:51,730 --> 01:20:52,460
here.

1612
01:20:52,460 --> 01:20:54,190
So basically, what
we really want

1613
01:20:54,190 --> 01:20:56,440
to develop as a
method is basically

1614
01:20:56,440 --> 01:20:59,320
[? those ?] that
continue to scale with

1615
01:20:59,320 --> 01:21:02,320
increased computation.

1616
01:21:02,320 --> 01:21:05,360
And when we want to
improve the AI agents,

1617
01:21:05,360 --> 01:21:11,055
we want to teach the model
to discover what we can,

1618
01:21:11,055 --> 01:21:13,580
not which contain what
we have discovered.

1619
01:21:13,580 --> 01:21:17,050
So it is more about
having some method

1620
01:21:17,050 --> 01:21:21,340
so that we can trigger
the model to know

1621
01:21:21,340 --> 01:21:25,870
how it can keep improving itself
and how to have the right way

1622
01:21:25,870 --> 01:21:28,240
to perform the reasoning.

1623
01:21:28,240 --> 01:21:30,795
So this is the end
of this lecture.

1624
01:21:30,795 --> 01:21:32,790
Thanks for listening.

